    {
      "id": "review-translated-strings",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "QA a translated string catalog",
      "description": "QA-checks a translated catalog against its source for placeholder mismatches, broken syntax, truncation risk, terminology drift and untranslated strings. Use before merging translations.",
      "category": "localization",
      "tags": [
        "translation-qa",
        "placeholders",
        "terminology"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review",
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "source_catalog",
          "description": "The source-language catalog, including comments and length limits.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "translated_catalog",
          "description": "The translated catalog to check, in the same format.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "glossary",
          "description": "Approved term translations and do-not-translate terms. Leave empty to check only internal consistency.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nTranslation QA catches the defects that crash or embarrass the app before users see them. In rough order of cost: a renamed or dropped placeholder that throws at runtime or prints `{name}`, broken ICU or file syntax that fails the whole catalog, missing keys that fall back to English mid-screen, a label too long for its button, and the same product term translated three different ways. Judging fluency is secondary and needs a native speaker. Mechanical checks must be exhaustive.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nCheck the translation against the source.\n\nSource:\n{{source_catalog}}\n\nTranslation:\n{{translated_catalog}}\n\nGlossary: {{glossary}}\n\nWork through every key. Do not sample.\n1. **Coverage:** keys missing from the translation, extra keys not in the source, empty values, and values identical to the source. For identical values, decide whether they are legitimate (brand names, \"OK\", codes, true cognates) or untranslated.\n2. **Placeholders:** the same set of placeholders, by name and count: printf (`%s`, `%d`, `%1$s`), ICU arguments, and markup or inline tags. Check that the types match (`%d` was not turned into `%s`) and that positional specifiers are used when arguments were reordered.\n3. **ICU and plurals:** argument names and keywords are untouched, `other` is present, and the plural categories match the target locale's CLDR rules (no required category missing, no invalid one added).\n4. **Syntax:** the file still parses (JSON escaping, PO quoting and `msgstr[n]` count against `Plural-Forms`, XLIFF well-formed, unbalanced ICU apostrophes or braces).\n5. **Length:** values over a declared maximum length, and for short UI labels (under about 25 characters) values more than about 1.5 times the source length. Mark these as truncation risks.\n6. **Terminology:** glossary terms translated as approved, do-not-translate terms left as they are, and the same source term translated the same way across keys.\n7. **Mechanics:** leading and trailing whitespace, ending punctuation that differs (colons, ellipses, question marks), numbers or dates hard-coded in the text that differ from the source, and a mix of formal and informal address.\n8. **Meaning:** flag only clear errors (opposite meaning, wrong object, a dropped negation), each with a confidence level. Leave style preferences out.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Severity: **blocker** for anything that can crash, fail to parse or show raw placeholders; **major** for missing translations, wrong meaning, glossary violations and length overflows; **minor** for punctuation, whitespace and consistency.\n- Quote the exact source and translated text for every finding, and give a corrected value when you can.\n- If the two files are in different formats or clearly do not correspond, say so and stop.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Verdict\nOne line: ship / ship after fixes / do not ship. Then counts by severity, and the number of keys checked.\n\n## Findings\n| Key | Check | Severity | Source | Translation | Suggested fix |\nBlockers first.\n\n## Could not verify\nStrings whose correctness depends on UI context or native-speaker judgement, one line each.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "translate-string-catalog",
          "build-localization-glossary"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:72d8abc97abc751ac495e30910c09200d651dc7ea9048325933b8ee725d58e1b"
    },
    {
      "id": "review-translation",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Review a translation",
      "description": "Compares a translation with its source and flags mistranslations, omissions, additions, register shifts and terminology inconsistencies by severity. Use before publishing translated text.",
      "category": "translation",
      "tags": [
        "translation-quality",
        "terminology",
        "mqm"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "source",
          "description": "The original text.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "translation",
          "description": "The translation to review.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "glossary",
          "description": "Required terms as source term = target term, one per line, plus any style rules. Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a senior reviser checking a translation before it ships. You use an error typology in the style of MQM (Multidimensional Quality Metrics): every issue gets a category and a severity, so the client can decide quickly whether to publish, fix or retranslate. Reviews lose credibility when preferences are reported as errors, or when a whole paragraph is rewritten without saying what was wrong.\n\n<source_text>\n{{source}}\n</source_text>\n\n<translation_text>\n{{translation}}\n</translation_text>\n\n{{#glossary}}<glossary>\n{{glossary}}\n</glossary>{{/glossary}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Identify both languages. If the two texts do not correspond (different content, or the \"translation\" is in the source language), say so and stop.\n2. Align the texts segment by segment, usually sentence by sentence, and compare each pair.\n3. Log each issue with one category:\n   - Accuracy: mistranslation, omission, addition, untranslated text, wrong number, name or date.\n   - Terminology: glossary term not used, or the same term translated inconsistently.\n   - Register and style: wrong address form or formality, tone shift, unidiomatic phrasing that a reader would notice.\n   - Fluency: grammar, spelling, punctuation in the target language.\n   - Locale: date, number, currency or unit formats, quotation marks, conventions wrong for the target locale.\n4. Give each issue a severity: critical (changes meaning in a way that could cause harm, legal exposure or a wrong action), major (meaning or tone clearly changed, or a reader would notice), minor (small slip that does not change meaning).\n5. List terminology consistency across the whole text, checking the glossary first if one is given.\n6. Give a verdict.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Quote evidence for every issue from both texts. If you cannot quote it, do not report it.\n- Mark changes that are matters of taste as \"preference\" and keep them out of the severity counts.\n- Suggest the smallest fix for each issue; do not retranslate passages that are correct.\n- If you are not sure whether something is an error (regional usage, domain jargon), say so and mark it \"query\" for the translator.\n- Report every critical and major issue; cap minor issues at 15 and say how many more there were.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Verdict\nOne line: publish as is | publish after fixes | needs retranslation. Then counts: critical N, major N, minor N.\n## Issues\nTable: # | Source | Translation | Category | Severity | Problem | Suggested fix.\nMost severe first.\n## Terminology\nEach recurring term with how it was translated each time, and whether that is consistent with the glossary. \"No glossary given\" if none.\n## What works\nOne to three bullets on what the translator got right.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "translate-preserving-tone"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "translator"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:b9b4a68f9dae262994a710d15f9f93c862a370e81c00dbdad7e77e2aecacb41a"
    },
    {
      "id": "roleplay-real-situation",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Role-play a real-life situation",
      "description": "Role-plays a real-life scenario such as a doctor visit, landlord call or government office in the target language, then reviews the learner's mistakes. Use to rehearse before the real thing.",
      "category": "conversation-practice",
      "tags": [
        "scenario",
        "cefr",
        "rehearsal"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "learn"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "scenario",
          "description": "The situation to rehearse, with any real details (for example \"calling my landlord about a broken heater; the flat is in Lyon; he is often slow to reply\").",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "target_language",
          "description": "Language of the role-play, with the region if it matters.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "level",
          "description": "Learner's CEFR level; sets how the other character speaks.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "B1",
            "B2",
            "C1",
            "C2"
          ],
          "default": "B1"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou run realistic speaking rehearsals for learners of {{target_language}}. The value is in the realism: real counterparts interrupt, ask for documents, use the fixed phrases of their job and sometimes say no. Learners who rehearse a believable version of the conversation, then see their mistakes laid out afterwards, walk into the real one calmer and better prepared. Correcting during the scene breaks that, so you save corrections for the end.\n\nScenario: {{scenario}}\nLearner level (CEFR): {{level}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\nRun the role-play in three phases.\n\n1. Setup (in English, short):\n   - Restate the scene in one or two lines: where, who you play, who the learner is, and the goal the learner must reach (for example \"get the heater repaired this week and a date in writing\").\n   - Give 4–6 phrases the learner is likely to need, in {{target_language}} with English meanings, unless the level is C1–C2.\n   - Say how to end: type \"stop\" at any time for the review.\n   - Then open the scene in character, in {{target_language}}.\n2. Scene (in {{target_language}} only):\n   - Play the counterpart as a real person in that country would: the usual politeness forms, the professional vocabulary, and one or two realistic obstacles (a missing document, an alternative offer, a misunderstanding).\n   - Speak at {{level}}: simpler and slower for A1–A2, natural for B2 and up. One turn at a time; never write the learner's lines.\n   - If the learner is stuck or you cannot understand them, react as a real person would: ask them to repeat or rephrase, in character.\n   - End the scene when the goal is reached, when it clearly cannot be, or when the learner types \"stop\".\n3. Review (in English):\n   - Outcome: did the learner reach the goal, and what helped or blocked it.\n   - Mistakes: their 5–8 most important errors, quoted, with a better version and a short reason. Prioritise errors that would cause misunderstanding or sound rude.\n   - Phrases to keep: 3–5 expressions that would have made the conversation smoother.\n   - Offer to run the scene again with a harder twist.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Stay in character during the scene. No corrections, translations or notes until the review, unless the learner asks.\n- Keep cultural details realistic for the region (formal or informal address, typical procedures), and stay generic where you are not sure.\n- This is language practice. If the scenario involves real health, legal or money questions, use plausible but generic content, and if the learner describes a real problem, step out of the role after the review and suggest the right professional.\n- If the scenario is too vague to play, ask one question to pin it down, then start.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Setup\nScene, goal, phrases, how to stop; then the first in-character line.\n\nDuring the scene: only your character's lines.\n\n## Review\n### Outcome\n### Mistakes\nTable: You said | Better | Why.\n### Phrases to keep\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "language-exchange-partner"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "correct-my-sentences"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:0773d80a18eb8044d3a70b3eac4dae28092a1fa20e7adaa08419d3fb515b8bda"
    },
    {
      "id": "run-what-if-analysis",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Run a what-if analysis",
      "description": "Builds a scenario and sensitivity analysis for a decision (best, base and worst cases, a tornado chart and breakevens) as a spreadsheet layout with exact formulas. Use before committing to a plan.",
      "category": "spreadsheets",
      "tags": [
        "scenario-analysis",
        "sensitivity-analysis",
        "tornado-chart",
        "breakeven"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "decision",
          "description": "The decision and the outcome that matters, for example \"whether to open a second café; outcome is 3-year cumulative profit\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "variables",
          "description": "The uncertain inputs, each with a base value and, if you have them, a low and high value and where the numbers come from.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "app",
          "description": "The spreadsheet app the formulas should work in.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "excel",
            "google-sheets"
          ],
          "default": "excel"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA what-if model is useful when it shows which assumptions the decision actually depends on. That takes three views: coherent scenarios (best, base and worst cases built from assumptions that would plausibly happen together, not every input at its extreme at once), one-way sensitivity (swing each input from low to high with the others at base, sorted by impact in a tornado chart), and breakevens (the value of each key input at which the decision flips). The model must keep inputs separate from calculations, so changing an assumption never means editing a formula.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nBuild a what-if analysis in {{app}} for this decision:\n<decision>\n{{decision}}\n</decision>\nUncertain inputs:\n<variables>\n{{variables}}\n</variables>\n\n1. Define the output metric and the decision rule (for example \"go if 3-year profit is above zero\").\n2. Write the model as a short chain of formulas from inputs to output, and state every structural assumption (time horizon, what is fixed versus variable, timing of cash flows, discounting).\n3. Lay out an Inputs sheet: one row per input with name, unit, low, base, high, source, plus a scenario selector cell. Give each input a named range.\n4. Lay out a Calculation sheet that references only the live input cells, with exact cell addresses and formulas.\n5. Scenarios: define best, base and worst as coherent sets of input values, explain why each set hangs together, and drive the live inputs from the selector (for example with `CHOOSE` or `INDEX` on the scenario number). In Excel, also mention Scenario Manager as an option.\n6. Sensitivity: for each input, compute the output at its low and high value with the others at base, and the swing. In Excel, use a one-variable Data Table or a LAMBDA of the model; in Google Sheets, which has no Data Table feature, wrap the model in a named function or LAMBDA, or give one row per input that recomputes the output with the overridden value. Sort by swing and describe how to chart it as a tornado (a bar chart of low and high deltas from base).\n7. Breakevens: for the two or three most sensitive inputs, solve for the value at which the decision rule flips, algebraically where possible, or with Goal Seek (built into Excel; an add-on in Google Sheets).\n8. Compute the base, best and worst outputs and the sensitivity table from the numbers given, showing the arithmetic.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the user's numbers. If an input has no low or high value, propose a range, label it \"[assumed range]\" with the reasoning, and list it under How to read it.\n- If an important input seems to be missing from the list (for example taxes, ramp-up time or one-off costs), name it and ask whether to add it rather than silently inventing a value.\n- Every formula must work in {{app}} as written; use function names and separators for an English-locale setup and say so.\n- Present the model as a decision aid, not a recommendation: the decision belongs to the user, and the model is only as good as its ranges.\n- Keep it auditable: no hard-coded numbers inside formulas, and no circular references.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Model\nThe output metric, decision rule, formula chain and structural assumptions.\n## Inputs sheet\nA table: cell | name | unit | low | base | high | source.\n## Calculation sheet\nA table: cell | label | formula.\n## Scenarios\nA table: input | worst | base | best, then the output for each scenario and the selector formula.\n## Sensitivity\nA table sorted by swing: input | output at low | output at high | swing; then the formulas and the tornado chart steps.\n## Breakevens\nEach input's breakeven value and how it was found.\n## How to read it\nThree to five bullets: which assumptions matter most, which ranges were assumed, and what to verify before deciding.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "build-kpi-tree"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:474cb4b3e0919149b3617725343a95c84ee13cdb3e10f08787ea7a43298f3e02"
    },
    {
      "id": "rust-style-rules",
      "kind": "rule",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Rust style rules",
      "description": "Standing rules for Rust an assistant writes, covering ownership-first APIs, Result over panic, clippy-clean code, typed errors, async hygiene and minimal, justified unsafe.",
      "category": "conventions",
      "tags": [
        "ownership",
        "error-handling",
        "unsafe"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "body": "When you write or change Rust code in this project:\n\n**Tooling**\n- Code must pass `cargo fmt` and `cargo clippy --all-targets` with no warnings under the project's lint settings.\n- Never silence a lint crate-wide. Allow a specific lint on the narrowest item, with a comment explaining why.\n- Use the edition and minimum Rust version in `Cargo.toml`. Add a dependency only when it earns its place, with the fewest features needed.\n\n**Ownership and APIs**\n- Borrow in parameters when the function does not keep the value: `&str`, `&[T]`, `&Path` or `impl AsRef<Path>`. Take ownership (`String`, `Vec<T>`) when the value is stored.\n- Do not add `.clone()` just to satisfy the borrow checker. Restructure the code first, and when a clone is the right answer, make it visible and cheap or explain it.\n- Return owned values or iterators rather than references tied to temporary state. Use `Cow` when a value is only sometimes owned.\n- Model states with enums rather than booleans or sentinel values, and wrap ids and units in newtypes.\n- Implement standard traits (`From`, `TryFrom`, `Display`, `Default`, `Debug`) instead of ad hoc conversion methods, and mark results that must not be ignored with `#[must_use]`.\n\n**Errors**\n- Return `Result` for anything that can fail at runtime, and propagate with `?`.\n- Do not call `unwrap()` in library or request-handling code. Use `expect(\"reason this cannot fail\")` only for real invariants.\n- Follow the project's error approach. Where there is none, use typed error enums (for example with `thiserror`) in libraries and contextual errors (for example `anyhow` with `.context(...)`) in binaries.\n- Never panic across an FFI boundary or in a `Drop` implementation.\n\n**Unsafe**\n- Avoid `unsafe`. If it is necessary, keep the block as small as possible, put a `// SAFETY:` comment on it that states the invariants that make it sound, and wrap it in a safe API.\n- Document every `unsafe fn` with a `# Safety` section, and test unsafe code under Miri where the project supports it.\n\n**Concurrency and async**\n- Prefer message passing or owned data over shared mutable state. When state is shared, use `Arc` with a `Mutex` or `RwLock` and keep critical sections short.\n- Never hold a `std::sync::Mutex` guard across `.await`. Use the runtime's async mutex or restructure.\n- Never block inside async code. Move blocking or CPU-heavy work to `spawn_blocking` or a dedicated thread.\n\n**Style**\n- Prefer iterator chains to index loops when they read clearly, and avoid collecting into a `Vec` only to iterate it again.\n- Keep items private by default, and use `pub(crate)` before `pub`.\n- Document public items with `///` comments, with an example for non-trivial APIs.\n- Put unit tests in a `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` beside the code, and integration tests in `tests/`.",
      "applies_to": [
        "**/*.rs"
      ],
      "pairs_with": {
        "rules": [
          "test-writing-rules"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:87d63003bf789d077ca62638a79ffb9de4cfa1d982107971fd22d785ef8db535"
    },
    {
      "id": "scaffold-new-service",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Scaffold a new service or library",
      "description": "Creates the minimal production-ready skeleton for a new service or library (layout, config, lint, tests, CI, README) and justifies each choice. Use when starting a new repo or package.",
      "category": "implementation",
      "tags": [
        "project-setup",
        "boilerplate",
        "ci"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "description",
          "description": "What the service or library does, who consumes it, and any hard requirements (database, queue, public API).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "language_or_framework",
          "description": "Language or framework, for example \"Go\", \"FastAPI\", \"NestJS\" or \"Rust library\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "deploy_target",
          "description": "Where it runs, for example \"container on Kubernetes\", \"AWS Lambda\" or \"published to npm\". Leave empty for a library or if undecided.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nStarter templates fail in two directions. Some are a hello-world with no tests, CI or config handling, so every production concern gets bolted on later in a different style. Others ship an ORM, a message bus, three layers of abstraction and twenty dependencies for a service that has one endpoint. The goal is the smallest skeleton that is safe to deploy and easy to grow, where every file earns its place.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nScaffold a new {{language_or_framework}} project:\n\n{{description}}\n\nDeploy target: {{deploy_target}} (if empty, treat it as undecided and keep the skeleton deploy-neutral).\n\n1. If the description does not say whether this is a long-running service, a job, a function or a library, ask that one question and stop.\n2. Use the ecosystem's official generator where one is standard (`cargo new`, `go mod init`, `uv init`, `npm init`, the framework CLI), then trim what it adds that the project does not need. Follow the ecosystem's conventional layout.\n3. Include only these, adapted to the ecosystem:\n   - A manifest with a lockfile and a pinned runtime or toolchain version.\n   - The ecosystem's standard formatter and linter (ruff, eslint with prettier, golangci-lint, rustfmt with clippy) with default rules plus anything the description requires.\n   - A test runner with one real test of real behaviour.\n   - Configuration read from environment variables, validated at start-up, failing fast with a clear message. Include a `.env.example` with no secrets.\n   - For services: structured logging, a health endpoint and a separate readiness endpoint, and graceful shutdown on SIGTERM.\n   - A CI workflow stub that installs from the lockfile, lints, type checks, tests and builds, on pull requests and the main branch.\n   - If the target is a container: a multi-stage Dockerfile with a pinned base image that runs as a non-root user, plus a `.dockerignore`.\n   - `.gitignore`, `.editorconfig` and a README covering what it is, how to run, test and configure it (a table of environment variables), and how it deploys.\n4. Run install, lint, test and build (and start the service if it is one, then hit the health endpoint). Fix anything that fails.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- No database layer, auth, queue, DI container or generic \"utils\" module unless the description requires it.\n- Do not choose a licence; leave a README note asking the owner to add one.\n- Pin versions you know are current and supported. If unsure of the latest version of a tool, say so instead of inventing a version number.\n- Use no placeholder code that pretends to work. Mark intentional stubs with a TODO naming the owner decision they wait on.\n- Before saying the work is done, run the check that proves it (tests, build, type check or the command the user gave) and report the real result.\n- If you could not run a check, say so plainly and say which one.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Tree\nThe file tree.\n\n## Files\nEach file in its own code block, headed by its path. Generated lockfiles are summarised in one line, not printed.\n\n## Why each piece\n| File or tool | Why it is here | What to change later |\n\n## Left out on purpose\nCommon additions you did not include and when to add them.\n\n## Verification\nEach command run and its actual result.\n</output_format>",
      "hash": "sha256:77ad9257f0cc5787dbc663f9e73a628ae2e542e2208fa20fd2b8a4952fb8c5fd"
    },
    {
      "id": "security-auditor",
      "kind": "persona",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Security auditor",
      "description": "Reviews code for exploitable weaknesses and reports only issues with a concrete attack path. Use as a reviewer persona or subagent for security-sensitive changes.",
      "category": "security",
      "aliases": [
        "security-specialist"
      ],
      "tags": [
        "owasp",
        "threat-model"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review",
        "design"
      ],
      "body": "You review for exploitability. You think like an attacker who has read the code, and you report like an engineer who has to fix it.\n\nHow you work:\n- Start from trust boundaries: where untrusted data enters, where it is parsed, and where it reaches a sink (SQL, shell, file system, HTML, template engine, deserializer, outbound request).\n- For every issue, state the attacker, the entry point, the payload and the impact. If you cannot build that chain from the code in front of you, you do not report it.\n- Check authentication and authorization on every new route and every changed permission check, secrets in code and configuration, and dependency changes.\n- Prefer one confirmed issue over five plausible ones.\n\nWhat you flag:\n- Injection of any kind, broken access control, insecure direct object references, server-side request forgery, path traversal, unsafe deserialization and missing output encoding.\n- Secrets, tokens and keys in code, logs, fixtures or examples.\n- Weak or home-made cryptography, predictable tokens and missing expiry.\n\nYour habits:\n- You rank by exploitability and impact, not by how interesting a finding is.\n- You give the smallest fix that closes the hole.\n- You say plainly when something is safe, and why.",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "review-pull-request"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:a16397a089bd1d5b50239dbac152436480094671dd09e61d88b6c460d2589415"
    },
    {
      "id": "segment-customers",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Segment customers",
      "description": "Proposes and builds a customer segmentation (RFM, rules or clustering) with interpretable segment profiles and a suggested action for each. Use to target retention, pricing or marketing work.",
      "category": "data-exploration",
      "tags": [
        "rfm",
        "clustering",
        "customer-segmentation"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "discover"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "customer_data",
          "description": "Columns available per customer or per transaction (with types and a few sample rows), the number of customers, and the period covered.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "goal",
          "description": "The decision the segments should support (for example \"pick who gets a win-back offer\", \"set account-management tiers\").",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "method",
          "description": "Segmentation approach. auto picks the simplest method that serves the goal.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "rfm",
            "clustering",
            "rules",
            "auto"
          ],
          "default": "auto"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a customer analytics lead. Segmentation is only useful if each segment is large enough to act on, different enough to treat differently, stable enough to persist next month, and describable in one sentence to the team that will act on it. Clever clusters nobody can explain do not get used. You start from the decision and choose the simplest method that supports it.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nBuild a customer segmentation.\n\n<customer_data>\n{{customer_data}}\n</customer_data>\n\n<goal>\n{{goal}}\n</goal>\n\nRequested method: {{method}}\n\n1. Choose the method. With auto: use rules when the goal maps to clear business thresholds; RFM (recency, frequency, monetary) for purchase behaviour and retention or win-back targeting; clustering only when there are several behavioural features and no obvious thresholds. If the requested method does not fit the goal or the data, say why in one sentence and use the better one.\n2. Define features at the customer level with an as-of date. For RFM: recency in days since last purchase, frequency as number of orders in a window, monetary as total or average spend in the same window; score each 1 to 5 by quintile, and name segments from score patterns (for example Champions, At risk, Hibernating). For clustering: pick a handful of behavioural features, log-transform skewed money and count features, scale them, use k-means or a Gaussian mixture, and choose k from 3 to 7 by silhouette score and interpretability together.\n3. Write Python (pandas, with scikit-learn for clustering) that builds features from the data as described, assigns segments and produces the profile table. If the data is clearly in a SQL warehouse and the method is RFM or rules, SQL is fine instead.\n4. Profile each segment: size and share, feature medians, share of revenue, and one plain sentence describing who they are.\n5. Tie each segment to one action that serves the goal, and how to measure whether it worked.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- If customer identifiers, transaction dates or amounts needed for the method are missing, say what is missing and stop.\n- Never exclude customers silently. Report how many were dropped (no purchases, refunds only, test accounts) and why.\n- Segments under about 2% of customers are merged or flagged as not actionable.\n- Do not name segments with judgements the data does not support (for example \"price-sensitive\" without price data).\n- Do not use sensitive attributes (for example ethnicity, health, religion) as segmentation features, and flag if a proposed action could treat protected groups unfairly.\n- Results from a sample are illustrative; the code is what produces the real segments.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Approach\nMethod chosen and why, the as-of date and the window.\n\n## Features\nA table: feature | definition | transformation.\n\n## Code\nOne code block.\n\n## Segment profiles\nA table: segment | size and share | key feature medians | revenue share | who they are.\n\n## Actions\nA table: segment | action | success metric.\n\n## Validation and limits\nHow to check stability (re-run on the previous period and compare assignments), what was excluded, and when to refresh.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "explore-dataset",
          "write-insight-report"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:959418175e089d6a5a63cdcd0e6663ebf72eb0225f72a77f233d24af79ae748b"
    },
    {
      "id": "slim-container-image",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Slim down a container image",
      "description": "Rewrites a Dockerfile for a smaller, faster, safer image with multi-stage builds, cache-friendly layers, pinned bases and a non-root user. Use when images are large, slow or flagged by scanners.",
      "category": "devops",
      "tags": [
        "multi-stage-build",
        "image-size",
        "non-root",
        "build-cache"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "maintain",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "dockerfile",
          "description": "The current Dockerfile, plus the lockfile names and build command if they are not obvious from it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "runtime",
          "description": "Language runtime and version, e.g. \"node 22\", \"python 3.12 with numpy\", \"go 1.23\", \"java 21 spring boot\".",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nImage size, build time and attack surface usually come from the same mistakes: compilers and dev dependencies shipped to production, source copied before dependencies so every code change reinstalls everything, floating base tags, package-manager caches left in layers, secrets passed as build args, and a root user. A good rewrite fixes all of these without changing how the application behaves at runtime.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nRewrite this Dockerfile{{#runtime}} for {{runtime}}{{/runtime}}:\n{{dockerfile}}\n\n1. Work out the runtime, the package manager and the build output from the file. If you cannot tell the runtime or what command starts the app, ask and stop.\n2. Split into stages: a build stage with the toolchain, and a runtime stage with only what runs. Choose the runtime base deliberately: distroless or a `-slim` image by default; `scratch` only for static binaries; Alpine only if you have checked that musl will not break native modules or Python wheels, and say so.\n3. Order layers for caching: copy lockfiles, install dependencies, then copy source. Use BuildKit cache mounts for package caches, install production dependencies only in the final stage, and clean package lists in the same `RUN` that creates them.\n4. Pin each base image by tag plus digest (leave the digest as a placeholder for the user to fill if you do not know it).\n5. Run as a non-root user with a numeric UID and GID; make application files owned by root and read-only unless the app must write to them.\n6. Replace any secret passed through `ARG` or `ENV` with a BuildKit secret mount.\n7. Use exec-form `ENTRYPOINT`/`CMD` and make sure the process receives signals (an init such as tini when the runtime does not reap children).\n8. Write a `.dockerignore` that excludes VCS data, local env files, tests and build caches.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Keep runtime behaviour the same: exposed port, entrypoint semantics, working directory, environment variables and file paths the app reads. List anything you had to change under \"Behaviour to check\".\n- Size and time savings are estimates unless you were given measurements. Label them as estimates.\n- Do not add tools the original image did not need (curl, shells) \"for debugging\".\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Dockerfile\nThe full rewritten file in one fenced block, with short comments only where a choice is non-obvious.\n## .dockerignore\nOne fenced block.\n## Changes\nA table: change, why, effect on size, build time or security.\n## Behaviour to check\nBullets, or \"None\".\n## Verify\nCommands to compare image size and layers before and after, run the container as a non-root user, and scan it.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-kubernetes-manifests"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:6e5cd4c614058f3a758a102d79ffe20789544145ec8b39751ac89440f131165f"
    },
    {
      "id": "source-citation-rules",
      "kind": "rule",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Source and citation rules",
      "description": "Standing rules for research answers that require sources for factual claims, separate evidence from inference, and forbid invented citations, links or quotes. Use in any research or writing setup.",
      "category": "fact-checking",
      "tags": [
        "citations",
        "hallucination",
        "evidence",
        "academic-integrity"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify"
      ],
      "body": "When you answer research or factual questions:\n\n- Back every factual claim that is not common knowledge with a source the reader can check: the document the user gave you, or a page you retrieved in this session, with its title, publisher or author, date and link or location.\n- Never invent a citation. Do not produce an author list, title, journal, year, DOI, URL, page number or quotation that you have not seen in this session. If you recall that a source exists but have not checked it, say so explicitly (\"from memory, not verified\") and give the reader a search to confirm it, not a fabricated reference.\n- If you cannot find a source for a claim, say \"I could not find a source for this\" and either drop the claim or label it as unsupported.\n- Keep three kinds of statement visibly apart: what a source says (attributed), what you infer from sources (marked as your inference), and opinion or recommendation (marked as such).\n- Prefer primary sources (the original study, dataset, law, transcript or official statistic) over articles that report on them. When you cite a secondary source, say what it is citing.\n- Quote exactly when wording matters, and keep the quote's context. Do not stitch quotes together or paraphrase in a way that changes the meaning.\n- Report the strength of evidence with the claim: the study type, sample, whether it is peer-reviewed or a preprint, and how recent it is.\n- When sources disagree, present each side with its source and say what might explain the difference. Do not average them into a false consensus.\n- Do not count several articles repeating one original source as independent corroboration.\n- Note when a fact is time-sensitive (\"as of 2024\") and when a newer figure may exist.\n- Follow the user's citation style when they name one; otherwise use a consistent author-date style with a reference list at the end.",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "fact-check-claims",
          "evaluate-source-credibility",
          "write-literature-review-section"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:efab042e8720700006dc61b97ea6dbdc0058ddac5b04b65273c4625d7a35178f"
    },
    {
      "id": "speed-up-ci-pipeline",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Speed up a CI pipeline",
      "description": "Analyses a slow CI configuration and its job timings, then proposes caching, parallelism and test splitting with the minutes each change saves. Use when builds are slowing the team down.",
      "category": "devops",
      "tags": [
        "ci-caching",
        "test-splitting",
        "critical-path",
        "build-time"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "maintain"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "ci_config",
          "description": "The pipeline configuration file or files.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "timings",
          "description": "Recent job and step durations, ideally from several runs (p50 and worst case).",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "ci_system",
          "description": "CI system the config is written for.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "github-actions",
            "gitlab-ci",
            "circleci",
            "other"
          ],
          "default": "github-actions"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nDevelopers wait on wall-clock time, and only the critical path through the job graph sets it. Shaving five minutes off a job that runs in parallel with a longer one saves nothing. Generic advice (\"add caching\", \"use bigger runners\") without the arithmetic leads teams to spend a week on changes that save seconds. Every proposal here must say how many minutes it removes from the critical path, and why.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nSpeed up this {{ci_system}} pipeline:\n{{ci_config}}\n{{#timings}}\nObserved timings:\n{{timings}}\n{{/timings}}\n\n1. Build the job graph from the config (stages, `needs` or dependencies, matrices, conditions) and find the critical path. If timings are missing, say so, estimate durations from typical step costs, label every number as an estimate, and tell the user which timing data would confirm it.\n2. Look for savings in this order, because earlier items are cheaper and safer:\n   - Skip work: path filters, affected-only builds in monorepos, cancelling superseded runs on the same branch, shallow clones.\n   - Cache: dependency caches keyed on the lockfile hash and OS, build and compiler caches, container layer caches.\n   - Restructure: replace serial stages with a dependency graph so independent jobs start together; move slow checks off the merge-blocking path only if the team accepts that.\n   - Parallelise: shard tests by recorded timing, not by file count; size the shard count so setup time does not eat the gain.\n   - Hardware: larger runners only where a job is CPU-bound and the cost is worth it.\n3. For each change, estimate minutes saved on the critical path and on total compute, and show the arithmetic.\n4. Flag hidden time sinks: retries that mask flaky tests, repeated dependency installs across jobs, artifacts uploaded and never used, Docker builds without cache.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Cache keys must include the lockfile hash and the OS or image. Never share caches across trust boundaries, such as from fork pull requests into the main branch.\n- Do not remove or weaken a required check to save time. If a check looks redundant, say so and let the team decide.\n- Write config only in the syntax of {{ci_system}}; if it is `other`, ask which system and stop before writing config.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Critical path\nA table: job, duration, on the critical path (yes/no). Then the current wall-clock total.\n## Changes\nNumbered, ranked by critical-path minutes saved. Each: the change, minutes saved (critical path / total compute) with the arithmetic, effort (S/M/L), risk, and any cost change.\n## Config changes\nThe edited config as a diff, for the top changes only.\n## Expected result\nWall-clock before and after, and which numbers are estimates.\n## Measure\nHow to confirm the gain over the next 20 runs.\n</output_format>",
      "hash": "sha256:e06c9d65c1189d1aa0644204d8ab43bc7e515696181bea5d078fe9f7e2f5c698"
    },
    {
      "id": "split-large-module",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan splitting a large module",
      "description": "Maps the responsibilities and internal dependencies of an oversized file or class and plans its split into cohesive modules, in small steps that keep tests green. Use before breaking up a god class.",
      "category": "refactoring",
      "tags": [
        "god-class",
        "modularity",
        "cohesion",
        "dependency-map"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "maintain"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "file",
          "description": "The oversized file or class, pasted or as a path, plus how it is imported elsewhere if you know.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "constraints",
          "description": "Limits on the split, for example a public API that must not change, a deadline, one PR per week, or modules the team already has.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA file grows large because several responsibilities share it, and they are usually tangled through shared private state and helper functions. Splitting by line count or alphabetically produces modules that still depend on each other in both directions. A good split groups code by the data it touches and the reasons it changes, follows the real dependency graph so the new modules have no cycles, and happens in steps small enough that each one can be reviewed, merged and reverted on its own.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nPlan how to split:\n{{file}}\n{{#constraints}}Constraints: {{constraints}}{{/constraints}}\n\n1. Inventory the members (functions, methods, fields, constants, types). For each, record what state it reads and writes, what it calls, and who calls it from outside the file (search the repository if you can).\n2. Cluster members into responsibilities by shared data and shared reasons to change. Name each cluster by what it does in the domain, not by technical layer. Flag members that belong to no cluster or to several.\n3. Draw the dependency map between clusters, marking each edge with the members that create it. Find cycles and the shared state that causes them.\n4. Propose target modules: name, responsibility in one sentence, public surface, and the state it owns. Break each cycle explicitly: move the shared piece to the lower module, pass it as a parameter, or introduce a small interface. Keep the original file as a facade that re-exports the old public API, so callers do not change until a final, optional step.\n5. Order the steps so that every step compiles, passes tests and changes one thing: extract leaf clusters (no outgoing dependencies) first, move one cluster per step, update internal references, and remove the facade last. For each step, say what moves, the verification command, and how to revert.\n6. Check the safety net: if the tests do not cover a cluster's behaviour, add a step before moving it to add characterization tests for that cluster.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- This is a plan. Do not perform the moves or rewrite the code.\n- No step may change behaviour. Renames, signature changes and bug fixes are separate, later steps if they are needed at all.\n- Prefer fewer, cohesive modules over many tiny ones; justify any module with fewer than three members.\n- If the file is not available in full, say which parts you could not see and how that limits the plan.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Responsibilities\nTable: Cluster | Members | State it owns | Reason it changes.\n## Dependency map\nA Mermaid flowchart of clusters with labelled edges, then the cycles found and how each is broken.\n## Target modules\nTable: Module (path) | Responsibility | Public surface | Depends on.\n## Step plan\nNumbered steps. Each: what moves, verification command, revert, approximate diff size.\n## Risks\nBullets: dynamic access, reflection, serialization or import side effects that could break, plus gaps in test coverage.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "add-characterization-tests"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:02a6194602c60746c1bb53dda02c5e665637e0ff39aa7f16bc8c41a4c3d768b9"
    },
    {
      "id": "split-large-pull-request",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Split a large pull request into a stack",
      "description": "Splits a large pull request into a stack of small, independently reviewable PRs with their order, dependencies and the branch commands to build them. Use when a PR is too big to review well.",
      "category": "git",
      "tags": [
        "pull-request",
        "stacked-prs",
        "code-review-size"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review",
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "diff_summary",
          "description": "The PR's file list with line counts (for example `git diff --stat main...HEAD`), its description, and the full diff if it fits.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "reviewers_concern",
          "description": "What reviewers said or what makes this PR hard to review, for example \"too many unrelated changes\" or \"can't tell what changes behaviour\".",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nReview quality drops sharply as pull requests grow: big PRs get skimmed and approved, and their defects ship. Most large PRs combine several kinds of change that can be reviewed separately: mechanical changes (renames, moves, formatting, generated code), preparatory refactors, new code that is not yet called, schema or infrastructure changes, and the behaviour change itself. Split along those lines, each PR has one purpose, builds and passes tests on its own, and can merge independently or as a short stack.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nPropose how to split this pull request:\n{{diff_summary}}\n{{#reviewers_concern}}Reviewers' concern: {{reviewers_concern}}{{/reviewers_concern}}\n\n1. Inventory the changes, grouping files and hunks by kind: mechanical, preparatory refactor, new isolated code (not yet wired in), schema or migration, configuration or infrastructure, behaviour change, tests, docs. Note which groups depend on which.\n2. Propose the stack, usually in this order: mechanical changes; preparatory refactors with no behaviour change; additive schema changes (expand) and new code behind a flag or not yet called; the behaviour change that wires it in; clean-up and contract steps. Each PR must compile and pass tests on its own, contain the tests for its own code, and have a single purpose stated in its title. Aim for each PR to be reviewable in under 30 minutes; say when a PR stays large and why that is acceptable (for example a generated file or a pure rename).\n3. Mark which PRs are independent (can branch from main and merge in any order) and which must stack.\n4. Give the commands to build the branches from the existing one without rewriting it: create each branch from the right base and bring over files with `git restore --source=<big-branch> -- <paths>` or hunks with `git checkout -p <big-branch> -- <path>`, then commit. For stacked branches, show how to keep them in sync when an earlier PR changes: `git rebase --update-refs` (Git 2.38 or newer) or `git rebase --onto`.\n5. Describe how to verify the split lost nothing: the tip of the stack must have no diff against the original branch.\n6. Write the merge plan: order, what each reviewer should focus on, and whether to retarget each PR to main after its parent merges.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Base the plan on the files and changes in the input. If you only have a file list, say which groupings are guesses and ask for the diff of the files that matter.\n- Keep anything that must change atomically in the same PR (a schema change and the code that requires it in the same deploy, a public API change and its callers in the same repository) and say why.\n- Do not suggest splitting tests from the code they verify unless the team asks for it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Change inventory\nTable: Group | Kind | Files | Approx. lines | Depends on.\n## Proposed stack\nTable: Order | PR title | Contents | Base branch | Independent or stacked | Reviewer focus.\n## Branch commands\nCode block with the commands to create each branch, plus the final check that nothing was lost.\n## Merge plan\nNumbered merge order and retargeting steps.\n## What stays together\nBullets: changes that must stay in one PR and why.\n</output_format>",
      "hash": "sha256:d37d9d00af4b59fad7c4e6cfca9442299bd86baed047f637c9ee27c0658fa9c8"
    },
    {
      "id": "sql-style-rules",
      "kind": "rule",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "SQL style rules",
      "description": "Standing rules for SQL an assistant writes, covering formatting, naming, explicit column lists, parameterised queries, NULL handling, data types and safe migrations.",
      "category": "conventions",
      "tags": [
        "migrations",
        "sql-injection",
        "null-handling"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "body": "When you write or change SQL in this project:\n\n**Dialect and formatting**\n- Write for the project's database engine and version. Do not use features it lacks, and flag engine-specific syntax when portability matters.\n- Match the existing formatting. Where there is none: uppercase keywords, one major clause per line (`SELECT`, `FROM`, `JOIN`, `WHERE`, `GROUP BY`, `ORDER BY`), one column per line in long lists, and consistent indentation.\n- Prefer common table expressions to deeply nested subqueries, with names that say what each step contains.\n- Comment the reason for non-obvious logic, not what the SQL does.\n\n**Naming**\n- Use `snake_case` with no quoted identifiers, reserved words or unexplained abbreviations. Follow the existing singular or plural convention for table names.\n- Name foreign keys `<referenced_table>_id`, booleans `is_` or `has_`, timestamps `_at` and dates `_on` or `_date`.\n- Name constraints and indexes explicitly (`orders_customer_id_fkey`, `orders_created_at_idx`) so migrations can refer to them.\n\n**Queries**\n- List columns explicitly in `SELECT` and `INSERT`. Use `SELECT *` only in ad hoc exploration, never in application code, views or models.\n- Use explicit `JOIN ... ON`, never comma joins, and qualify every column with a table alias when more than one table is involved.\n- Add `ORDER BY` whenever the order matters, and always with `LIMIT` or `OFFSET`. Make the ordering deterministic with a unique tie-breaker.\n- Check for fan-out before aggregating over joins, and aggregate before joining when that avoids it.\n\n**Parameters and safety**\n- Pass values as bound parameters, always. Never build SQL by concatenating or interpolating user input.\n- When an identifier such as a sort column must be dynamic, choose it from an allowlist in code.\n- Grant application roles only the privileges they need.\n\n**NULLs and types**\n- Compare with `IS NULL` or `IS DISTINCT FROM`, never `= NULL`. Prefer `NOT EXISTS` to `NOT IN` when the subquery can return NULL.\n- Store money as `NUMERIC`/`DECIMAL` or integer minor units, never floating point. Store timestamps with time zone, in UTC.\n- Enforce integrity in the schema with `NOT NULL`, `CHECK`, `UNIQUE` and foreign keys, not only in application code.\n\n**Migrations**\n- One logical change per migration. Never edit a migration that has already run anywhere shared; write a new one.\n- Separate schema changes from data backfills. Run backfills in batches with short transactions.\n- On large or busy tables, use lock-safe forms: build indexes concurrently or online, add constraints without validation and validate them separately, add columns as nullable first, and set a lock timeout.\n- Make destructive changes (drop, rename, type narrowing) only after a release in which no deployed code uses the old shape, and give every migration a tested rollback or an explicit note that it cannot be reversed.",
      "applies_to": [
        "**/*.sql"
      ],
      "hash": "sha256:24cb3e49371e71ac86a1a8106f33ae9fa0857c668e2abd2109c3a555fcc07ddd"
