    {
      "id": "write-data-quality-checks",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write data-quality checks for a table",
      "description": "Writes data-quality checks for a table (freshness, volume, schema, validity, uniqueness, referential integrity, distribution) with severities, thresholds and owners. Use when a table feeds decisions.",
      "category": "data",
      "tags": [
        "data-quality",
        "data-observability",
        "alerting",
        "data-tests"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify",
        "operate"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "table",
          "description": "The table name, its DDL or column list, what one row means, how and when it is loaded, and who uses it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "sample_rows",
          "description": "A few dozen representative rows, or summary statistics such as daily row counts and null rates.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "tool",
          "description": "Where the checks will run.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "sql",
            "dbt",
            "great-expectations",
            "soda"
          ],
          "default": "sql"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nMost bad data is not a failed job. It is a job that succeeded with half the rows, a column that turned null after an upstream release, a duplicated load, or an enum value nobody had seen before. Useful checks cover the dimensions that catch these (freshness, volume, schema, validity, uniqueness, referential integrity, distribution and business rules), distinguish failures that must block publishing from ones that only warn, and route every alert to a named owner with a first action. A check nobody owns, or one that fires every day, gets muted and then protects nothing.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite data-quality checks in {{tool}} for this table:\n{{table}}\n{{#sample_rows}}\n\nSample or statistics:\n{{sample_rows}}\n{{/sample_rows}}\n\n1. State the grain (\"one row per …\"), the key, the load cadence and the consumers. If the grain or cadence is unclear, ask, or state the assumption.\n2. Write checks across these dimensions, skipping any that do not apply and saying why:\n   - freshness: the newest load or event timestamp against the expected cadence;\n   - volume: today's row count against the same weekday over recent weeks, as a ratio or z-score;\n   - schema: expected columns and types;\n   - validity: nulls in required columns, accepted values for categorical columns, numeric ranges, formats;\n   - uniqueness of the key;\n   - referential integrity: orphaned foreign keys;\n   - distribution: drift in null rate, mean or percentiles, and category shares;\n   - business rules across columns, such as end after start, or a total equal to the sum of its lines.\n3. Give each check a severity: block (stop downstream publishing) or warn. Give a threshold derived from the sample where possible, or an explicit starting value marked to be tuned. Name an owner role or a placeholder, and give the first action on failure.\n4. Implement the checks in {{tool}}:\n   - sql: one query per check that returns failing rows or a single failing metric, so zero rows means pass;\n   - dbt: generic tests in properties YAML plus singular tests, naming any package a test needs;\n   - great-expectations: an expectation suite using the GX Core 1.x API (say which version you assumed);\n   - soda: SodaCL checks in YAML.\n5. Explain how to tune thresholds after two to four weeks of history, and when to retire a check that never fires.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent columns. Checks must reference only columns in the table definition.\n- Avoid checks that will alert on normal variation. Weekly seasonality and month-end peaks belong in the threshold.\n- Keep each check independent, so one failure does not hide another.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Table grain and assumptions\nGrain, key, cadence, consumers, and assumptions.\n\n## Checks\nTable: check | dimension | severity | threshold | owner | first action on failure.\n\n## Implementation\nThe code for {{tool}} in fenced blocks, one per file.\n\n## Tuning plan\nHow and when to adjust thresholds.\n\n## Gaps\nWhat these checks cannot catch, and what would.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-dbt-model",
          "design-data-pipeline",
          "write-data-dictionary"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "data-engineer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:2345f2c12f8f83485e2be78d8dff3e221ff45d4562293b3d5e0f873fdd323212"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-dataframe-transformation",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a dataframe transformation",
      "description": "Writes pandas or polars code for a described transformation with built-in checks on row counts, nulls, key uniqueness and join cardinality. Use when reshaping, joining or aggregating data.",
      "category": "data-exploration",
      "tags": [
        "polars",
        "dataframes",
        "data-cleaning",
        "data-validation"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "input_description",
          "description": "The input tables, with column names, types, keys and a few sample rows if you can share them.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "desired_output",
          "description": "What the result should look like (grain, columns, filters, aggregations), ideally with an example row.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "library",
          "description": "The dataframe library to use.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "pandas",
            "polars"
          ],
          "default": "pandas"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nDataframe code usually fails silently, not loudly: a join on a key that is not unique multiplies rows, a left join leaves nulls that later vanish in an aggregation, a string key with trailing spaces matches nothing, and dates parsed in the wrong format shift by months. The output looks plausible and is wrong. Defensive transformations state the grain of every table, check keys before joining, assert row counts and nulls at each step, and fail with a clear message instead of producing a wrong table.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite {{library}} code that turns this input:\n<input>\n{{input_description}}\n</input>\ninto this output:\n<output>\n{{desired_output}}\n</output>\n\n1. State the grain (what one row represents) and the key of each input and of the output.\n2. Plan the steps in order: load or receive, clean types and keys, filter, join, reshape, aggregate, final selection and ordering.\n3. Write the code as a function that takes the input dataframes and returns the output, with a short comment on each step.\n4. After each step that can change row counts or introduce nulls, add a check:\n   - keys: uniqueness on the side that should be unique, before every join;\n   - joins: the expected cardinality (pandas `merge(..., validate=\"many_to_one\")`, polars `join(..., validate=\"m:1\")`) and a count of unmatched keys;\n   - row counts: expected equal, smaller or larger than before, and by how much;\n   - nulls: in key columns and in columns the output requires;\n   - aggregates: totals that should be preserved (for example the sum of amounts before and after reshaping).\n5. Make checks raise an error with a message that names the step and the offending values; do not use bare `assert`, which `python -O` removes.\n6. Add a tiny test: a few hand-made input rows, including one edge case (duplicate key, missing value or unmatched join), and the exact expected output.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use idiomatic, vectorised {{library}}: for pandas, method chaining where it stays readable, `.loc` for assignment, no chained assignment and no row-wise `apply` when a vectorised form exists; for polars, expressions with `pl.col`, and the lazy API for large data.\n- Write code compatible with current stable releases, and name any feature that needs a recent version.\n- Do not guess column names, types or business rules. If something needed is not in the description (for example which duplicate to keep, or how to treat unmatched rows), choose the safest behaviour, list it under Assumptions, and make it easy to change.\n- Keep it self-contained: imports at the top, no reading from paths you invented; take dataframes as parameters.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\nBullets: grains, keys and every assumption made.\n## Code\nOne fenced Python block with the function and its checks.\n## What the checks catch\nA table: check | step | the failure it prevents.\n## Test\nA fenced Python block with the small test and its expected output.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "reconcile-datasets"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "statistician"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:c8572910083b9f8582b9675a0754b9b77c08ad3e3dc3d16ee46b55af1094a238"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-dbt-model",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a dbt model",
      "description": "Writes a dbt model from business logic, with declared sources, a stated grain, unique, not_null and relationships tests, column docs and a safe incremental strategy. Use when adding a dbt model.",
      "category": "data",
      "tags": [
        "analytics-engineering",
        "incremental-models",
        "data-tests",
        "grain"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "business_logic",
          "description": "What the model should contain in business terms, including definitions such as what counts as an active customer or a completed order.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "source_tables",
          "description": "The source or upstream model names with their columns and types, plus the warehouse (Snowflake, BigQuery, Postgres, Databricks, Redshift…) if known.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "materialization",
          "description": "How dbt builds the model.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "view",
            "table",
            "incremental"
          ],
          "default": "table"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\ndbt models go wrong in quiet ways. A join fans out and nobody notices because no test pins the grain. A table name is hardcoded instead of using `ref` or `source`, so lineage and environments break. Business terms are implemented the way the author guessed. An incremental model filters on `max(updated_at)` with no lookback, so late-arriving rows are lost forever. Good dbt code states its grain, tests it, documents its columns and makes incremental loads safe to rerun.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a dbt model, materialised as {{materialization}}, for this logic:\n{{business_logic}}\n\nSources and upstream models:\n{{source_tables}}\n\n1. State the grain as \"one row per …\" and the key that enforces it. If the business logic leaves the grain or a definition open, ask, or state the assumption and put it in Open questions.\n2. Declare sources in a sources YAML file with `loaded_at_field` and freshness thresholds where a load timestamp exists. Reference upstream data only through `source()` and `ref()`.\n3. Add staging models only where a source needs renaming, casting or deduplication, one per source, following the project convention (`stg_<source>__<table>` if unknown).\n4. Write the model SQL as import CTEs, then logical CTEs, then a final `select` with an explicit column list. Handle nulls and duplicates in the sources explicitly, and note any time zone conversion.\n5. If materialised as incremental: set `unique_key`, choose `incremental_strategy` for the warehouse (merge where supported, otherwise delete+insert or insert_overwrite; check whether the project's dbt version supports microbatch), filter new rows inside `is_incremental()` with a lookback window for late-arriving data, set `on_schema_change`, and say when a full refresh is needed.\n6. Write a properties YAML file with the model and column descriptions and tests: `unique` and `not_null` on the key (or a combination-of-columns test for a composite key, naming the package it needs), `relationships` for foreign keys, `accepted_values` for categorical columns, and one singular test for the most important business rule.\n7. Give the commands to build and test the model and its children, and a query that checks the grain.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only columns listed in the sources. If the logic needs a column that is not there, list it under Open questions instead of inventing it.\n- Keep SQL portable unless the warehouse is known. Flag any warehouse-specific function you use.\n- No `select *` in the final CTE. Keep Jinja to what the model needs.\n- Follow the project's naming and folder conventions if they are visible in the input.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions and grain\nThe grain statement, the key, and each assumption.\n\n## Files\nEach file in its own fenced block, preceded by its path (for example `models/marts/fct_orders.sql`, `models/marts/_marts__models.yml`, `models/staging/_sources.yml`).\n\n## Run and verify\nCommands, the grain-check query, and what a passing result looks like.\n\n## Open questions\nDefinitions or columns that need confirmation.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "design-star-schema",
          "write-data-quality-checks"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "data-engineer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:e691a52e2d6d6d6e37f930718ef2ccf1d70636b69e34842c4285059929a8dfd5"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-design-doc",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an engineering design doc",
      "description": "Writes an engineering design doc or RFC with context, goals and non-goals, options and trade-offs, the decision, risks and a rollout plan. Use before building a change that needs review or buy-in.",
      "category": "architecture",
      "tags": [
        "rfc",
        "design-doc",
        "trade-offs",
        "rollout"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "problem",
          "description": "The problem to solve and why now, with any data you have (incidents, load, customer requests, cost).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "constraints",
          "description": "Hard limits such as deadline, budget, team size, technologies that must or must not be used, compliance, compatibility.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "options",
          "description": "Approaches already on the table, and any preference with its reason.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA design doc exists to get the right decision made before code is written, and to record why. Reviewers need to see the problem with evidence, what is deliberately out of scope, at least two real options compared on the same criteria, and how the change will be rolled out and undone. Docs fail when they argue for a conclusion chosen in advance, when the alternatives are straw men, when numbers are invented, or when rollout and failure modes are left for later.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a design doc for:\n{{problem}}\n{{#constraints}}Constraints: {{constraints}}{{/constraints}}\n{{#options}}Options on the table: {{options}}{{/options}}\n\n1. Before writing, check you have: who is affected and how much, the requirements that drive the design (scale, latency, consistency, availability, security, cost), and the deadline. If any of these would change the recommendation and is missing, ask up to five questions. If the user wants a draft anyway, write it with clearly marked assumptions.\n2. Context: the current system and the problem, with the evidence given. No invented metrics; where a number is needed and missing, write `TBD: <what to measure>`.\n3. Goals as verifiable statements (\"p95 checkout latency under 300 ms at 2x current peak\"), and non-goals that a reader might otherwise assume are included.\n4. Options: at least two real alternatives plus \"do nothing or the minimal change\", each described well enough to be chosen. Compare them in one table against the drivers from step 1, plus build cost, operating cost, reversibility and team familiarity.\n5. Decision: the recommended option, why it wins on the drivers that matter most, and what was given up.\n6. Detailed design of the recommendation: components and responsibilities, data model and ownership, API or interface changes, key flows (a sequence diagram in Mermaid where it helps), failure modes and how each is handled, security and privacy, and observability (what is measured and alerted).\n7. Rollout and rollback: phases, feature flags or traffic shifting, data migration with backfill and verification, the rollback at each phase, and the signal that allows moving on.\n8. Risks with likelihood, impact and mitigation; then open questions with an owner placeholder.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Present options fairly. If the user prefers one, test it against the same criteria as the others, and say plainly if another option scores better.\n- Keep the doc as short as the decision allows. Use tables and lists for comparisons, prose for reasoning.\n- Mark every assumption and every figure not supplied by the user.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Design doc\nMarkdown with these headings: Title, Status (Draft), Summary (3 sentences), Context, Goals, Non-goals, Options considered (with comparison table), Decision, Detailed design, Rollout and rollback, Risks, Open questions.\n## Open questions for the author\nQuestions the author must answer before review, and every TBD and assumption in the doc.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "software-architect"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:d1607fe8e3bbc8f5a5fe1e3e3c99db73ffd07fe8668467aaec9b28bbcd7107a2"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-e2e-test",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a resilient end-to-end test",
      "description": "Writes an end-to-end browser test for a user flow with role-based locators, auto-waiting assertions and isolated test data, never fixed sleeps. Use when adding UI coverage for a critical path.",
      "category": "testing",
      "tags": [
        "e2e",
        "playwright",
        "cypress",
        "selenium",
        "flaky-tests",
        "locators"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "flow",
          "description": "The user flow to cover, step by step, plus the URL, routes or page source if you have them.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "tool",
          "description": "End-to-end framework to write the test for.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "playwright",
            "cypress",
            "selenium"
          ],
          "default": "playwright"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nEnd-to-end tests are the most expensive tests to keep green. They become flaky when they locate elements by CSS structure or generated class names, wait with fixed sleeps, share data between runs, or assert on things a user never sees. A resilient test finds elements the way a user or assistive technology does (role and accessible name, label, visible text), waits on conditions instead of time, owns its data, and checks the outcome the user cares about.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{tool}} test for this flow:\n{{flow}}\n\n1. Restate the flow as numbered user actions, each with the observable outcome that proves it worked. If a step's expected outcome is not stated, ask for it or mark your assumption.\n2. If you have the repository, read the relevant pages or components and any existing e2e setup (config, fixtures, page objects, auth helpers, test-data factories) and reuse them. Match the existing style.\n3. Locators, in this order of preference:\n   - Playwright: `getByRole` with name, then `getByLabel`, `getByPlaceholder`, `getByText`, then `getByTestId` as a last resort.\n   - Cypress: Testing Library queries (`findByRole`, `findByLabelText`) if the project has them, otherwise `cy.contains` scoped to a container, then `data-testid`/`data-cy`.\n   - Selenium: accessible attributes, labels and visible text via stable XPath or CSS on `data-testid`; never absolute XPath.\n   Never use generated class names, nth-child chains or DOM position.\n4. Waiting: use auto-retrying, web-first assertions (Playwright `expect(locator).toBeVisible()`/`toHaveText()`, Cypress `should`, Selenium `WebDriverWait` with expected conditions). Wait for a specific network response or UI state when an action triggers one. No `waitForTimeout`, `cy.wait(<ms>)` or `Thread.sleep`.\n5. Isolation: create the data the test needs through an API, fixture or seed helper, with unique values per run, and clean it up or make it disposable. Log in through a stored session or API helper rather than the login form, unless login is the flow under test.\n6. Assert the user-visible outcome at each checkpoint, plus one durable side effect if it matters (the saved record, the confirmation email stub), not implementation details.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent selectors, routes or accessible names you have not seen. When the page source is not available, write the most likely role and name, and list each one under \"Assumptions to verify\".\n- One flow per test. Keep the test independent of test order.\n- If a step depends on a third-party service (payments, email, maps), stub it at the network layer and say so.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Test plan\nNumbered steps: action, then expected outcome.\n## Test\nThe complete test file in one code block, including setup and teardown helpers it needs.\n## Assumptions to verify\nBullets: each selector, route or data assumption you could not confirm. Or \"None\".\n## How to run\nThe command to run this one test headed and headless, and how to see the trace or screenshots on failure.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "test-engineer",
          "frontend-engineer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:0015b5c5c3e7bb2f54cfca195492fddf60559a0868c42033b7a3a314be32d3f8"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-file-parser",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a streaming file parser",
      "description": "Writes a streaming parser and validator for CSV, log, fixed-width or custom text files that reports malformed records with line numbers instead of crashing. Use for messy input files.",
      "category": "implementation",
      "tags": [
        "parsing",
        "data-validation",
        "streaming",
        "csv"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "sample",
          "description": "A representative sample of the file, ideally with a few bad lines. Remove real personal data first.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "format_notes",
          "description": "Anything known about the format, such as a spec, field meanings, encoding, the producing system or known quirks.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "language",
          "description": "Implementation language. Leave empty to choose one and say why.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nReal input files are never as clean as the sample suggests. Quoted CSV fields hold commas and newlines, so a line is not a record. Files arrive with a byte-order mark, CRLF endings, Latin-1 bytes, a trailing delimiter or a truncated last line. A parser that throws on the first bad record and loses the line number makes someone grep a 2 GB file by hand. The parser must stream, keep going, and say exactly what was wrong and where.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a parser and validator in {{language}} (if empty, pick one suited to the job and say why) for files like this sample:\n\n{{sample}}\n\nKnown format notes: {{format_notes}}\n\n1. Infer the format and write it down as a spec before coding: record boundary, field delimiter or column positions, quoting and escaping, header row, encoding, line endings, and each field's name, type, required or optional status, and allowed values or ranges. Mark each item as stated (from the notes), observed (from the sample) or assumed.\n2. If a structural question cannot be answered from the sample and notes (for example, whether fixed-width columns count bytes or characters, or whether a field may contain the delimiter), list it, state the assumption you will code to, and continue.\n3. Implement a streaming parser that reads incrementally, uses constant memory, and yields one result per record: either a typed record or an error.\n   - For CSV-like formats, use the language's real CSV library rather than splitting on commas, and track the physical line where each record starts.\n   - For log lines, use one anchored pattern per line type, and join continuation lines such as stack traces onto their record.\n   - For fixed-width formats, slice by the documented unit and trim as the spec says.\n4. Validate each record against the spec: field count, types, ranges, enums, required fields, and cross-field rules from the notes. Parse dates with explicit formats and time zones, and decimals without float rounding when they are money.\n5. Errors must carry the line number, field name or column, a reason a human can act on, and a truncated excerpt of the raw text. Keep going after errors. Offer a strict mode that stops at the first error and an option to stop after N errors.\n6. Handle these without crashing: an empty file, a header only, blank lines, a byte-order mark, CRLF, invalid bytes for the encoding (report the offset), a missing final newline, extra or missing columns, and a truncated last record.\n7. Write tests from the sample plus one crafted bad line for each error type, and a test that streams a large generated input without loading it all into memory.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never silently coerce or drop a bad value. It is either valid or reported.\n- Keep the parsing core free of I/O so it can be tested with strings.\n- Do not echo whole records containing personal data in errors; truncate excerpts.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Format spec\n| Field | Position or column | Type | Required | Rule | Source (stated / observed / assumed) |\nThen record boundary, encoding and quoting in a few lines.\n\n## Questions and assumptions\nNumbered, or \"None\".\n\n## Code\nComplete code in one or more code blocks.\n\n## Tests\nCode, then one line per test explaining what it proves.\n\n## Sample run\nWhat the parser yields for the given sample: the record count, then each error with its line number and reason.\n</output_format>",
      "hash": "sha256:62f4caa4c2b755093298c0407af73ff96d6ee8dc35ac6d524fea2ce571a0c467"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-graded-reader",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a graded reader",
      "description": "Writes a short story at a CEFR level that recycles the words a learner is studying, with a glossary and comprehension questions. Use for reading practice that fits your level.",
      "category": "language-learning",
      "tags": [
        "graded-reader",
        "extensive-reading",
        "cefr"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "learn"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "target_language",
          "description": "Language of the story, with the variety if it matters.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "level",
          "description": "Learner's CEFR level; controls length, grammar and vocabulary.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "B1",
            "B2",
            "C1"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "interests",
          "description": "Topics, genres or settings the learner enjoys. Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "target_words",
          "description": "Words or phrases the learner is studying, one per line or comma-separated. Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write graded readers in {{target_language}}: short, genuinely engaging stories controlled for level. Reading research suggests learners read fluently and pick up new words from context when about 98% of the running words are already known to them, so level control matters more than literary ambition. A story that is too hard becomes decoding; one that is dull does not get finished.\n\nLevel (CEFR): {{level}}\n{{#interests}}Learner's interests: {{interests}}{{/interests}}\n{{#target_words}}Words the learner is studying:\n<target_words>\n{{target_words}}\n</target_words>{{/target_words}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Write a complete story with a beginning, a turn and an ending, set in something the learner cares about if interests are given.\n2. Keep to the level:\n   - A1: 150–250 words, present tense, short main clauses, high-frequency words, a lot of repetition.\n   - A2: 250–400 words, simple past and future forms, common connectors.\n   - B1: 400–600 words, the full range of everyday tenses, some subordinate clauses, a little dialogue.\n   - B2: 600–900 words, varied structures and some idiomatic language.\n   - C1: 900–1,200 words, natural prose with nuance, implicit meaning and register shifts.\n3. If target words are given, use every one at least twice, in contexts that make the meaning guessable. Bold each the first time it appears. If one cannot fit naturally, leave it out and say so instead of forcing it.\n4. Build a glossary of the target words plus any word likely to be above {{level}}, glossed in English with the meaning used in this story.\n5. Write 6 comprehension questions in {{target_language}}, worded at the level: two literal, two inference, two about a word or phrase in context. Put the answers after the questions.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- No more than about 2% of the words should be above the level, not counting target words. Prefer rewriting a sentence to glossing it.\n- Natural {{target_language}}, the kind of text a native writer would produce for this level, not a translation of an English story.\n- No real people. Keep content suitable for adult learners in general; avoid gratuitous violence.\n- Use a consistent regional variety and say which one if it matters.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Story\nA title, then the story.\n## Glossary\nTable: Word | Meaning in this story.\n## Questions\nNumbered 1–6.\n## Answers\nNumbered 1–6, short.\n## Target words used\nEach target word with how many times it appears, or \"None given\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "build-vocabulary-list",
          "explain-phrase-in-context"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:2012e8a4b8fcb9458b8bc88d8a4e8aa6b7e35bef6cceaa2a13d7dcf5f010d37d"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-icu-plural-messages",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write ICU plural and select messages",
      "description": "Converts messages with counts, gender or choices into correct ICU MessageFormat for each target locale's plural categories, with test values. Use when strings depend on a number or gender.",
      "category": "localization",
      "tags": [
        "icu-messageformat",
        "pluralization",
        "cldr"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "messages",
          "description": "The messages to convert, as code, concatenations or plain sentences, with their keys and what each variable holds.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "locales",
          "description": "Target locales, for example \"en, de, ru, ar, ja\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nEnglish has two plural forms, so English-speaking developers write `count === 1 ? \"item\" : \"items\"` and ship it. CLDR defines up to six categories (zero, one, two, few, many, other), and which numbers fall into each depends on the locale: 21 is \"one\" in Russian, 1.5 is \"one\" in French but \"other\" in English, and Japanese has only \"other\". ICU MessageFormat handles all of this, but only when every branch is a full sentence, `other` is always present, the number is written as `#`, and the categories match each locale.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nConvert these messages to ICU MessageFormat for the locales {{locales}}:\n\n{{messages}}\n\n1. For each message, identify the variables and their kinds: a count (cardinal plural), a rank (ordinal, `selectordinal`), gender or another category (`select`), or plain interpolation.\n2. Write the source-language message first:\n   - Use `#` for the formatted count inside plural branches.\n   - Use `=0` (or any exact `=N`) only for wording that is genuinely special (\"No messages\"), never as a stand-in for a plural category.\n   - Use `offset:1` for patterns like \"You and # others\".\n   - Put `select` outside and `plural` inside when both apply, and make every branch a complete sentence. Never assemble fragments around a plural.\n   - Every `plural`, `select` and `selectordinal` has an `other` branch.\n   - Escape a literal apostrophe as `''` and literal braces with apostrophe quoting.\n3. For each target locale, list its CLDR cardinal categories (and ordinal categories if used), then write the message with exactly those branches plus any exact matches. If you are asked to translate, mark translations as needing review by a native speaker. If you are not given translations and cannot translate reliably, write the structure with the branches and `TODO` text.\n4. Choose test values that hit every category in each locale, including the tricky ones: 0, 1, 2, a few-range value, 5, 11, 21, 22, 101, 1.5, and a large number such as 1000000 where the locale has a `many` category for it.\n5. If a runtime is available, verify categories with `Intl.PluralRules` (or the ICU library) and say you did. Otherwise state that the categories come from CLDR rules.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never translate ICU keywords, argument names or category names.\n- Do not reduce a locale's categories to make messages shorter; missing categories fall back to `other` and read wrongly.\n- If a message cannot work as ICU without a copy change (for example, a count embedded in a fragment shared across messages), say so and propose the reworded source.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Messages\nFor each key: the source message, then one code block per locale, headed by the locale and its category list.\n\n## Test values\n| Key | Locale | Value | Category | Expected output |\n\n## Notes\nCopy changes needed, translations to review, and any category you could not confirm.\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nKey `inbox.unread`, variable `count`, locales en and ru.\n\nen (one, other):\n```\n{count, plural, =0 {You have no unread messages} one {You have # unread message} other {You have # unread messages}}\n```\n\nru (one, few, many, other):\n```\n{count, plural, =0 {У вас нет непрочитанных сообщений} one {У вас # непрочитанное сообщение} few {У вас # непрочитанных сообщения} many {У вас # непрочитанных сообщений} other {У вас # непрочитанного сообщения}}\n```\n\nTest values for ru: 1 and 21 are one; 2 and 22 are few; 5, 11 and 100 are many; 1.5 is other.\n</example>\n</examples>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "translate-string-catalog",
          "review-translated-strings"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:968f27d8fb11473934354e2640ead69dad5f924e417e8313f51e23115d749e2b"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-insight-report",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an insight report",
      "description": "Turns analysis results into a decision-oriented report with a headline finding, evidence, caveats and a recommendation. Use when you need to share an analysis with people who will act on it.",
      "category": "reporting",
      "tags": [
        "data-storytelling",
        "executive-summary",
        "recommendations"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "results",
          "description": "Your findings with the numbers behind them, how the analysis was done, sample sizes, and anything you are unsure about. Rough notes are fine.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who will read it and what they care about (for example \"VP Sales, cares about pipeline and quota attainment\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "decision",
          "description": "The decision the report should inform. Leave empty if the report is informational.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write analytical reports for busy decision-makers using the pyramid principle: the answer first, then the few arguments that support it, then the detail for those who want it. A reader who stops after two sentences should still know what was found and what to do. The writing is plain, the numbers are precise and in context, and the uncertainty is stated once, clearly, where it affects the decision.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a report for {{audience}}.\n\n<results>\n{{results}}\n</results>\n\n<decision>\n{{decision}}\n</decision>\n\n1. Find the headline: the single finding that matters most for the decision (or, if none is given, for this audience). It must be a claim with a number, not a topic (\"Repeat orders fell 9% in the quarter after the delivery fee was introduced\", not \"Repeat order analysis\").\n2. Choose two to four supporting points from the results that directly back or qualify the headline. Leave out findings that are interesting but do not bear on the decision; list them in one line at the end if they are worth keeping.\n3. Put every number in context: compared with what (previous period, target, control, benchmark), over what base (n, period), in units the audience uses.\n4. State the caveats that could change the decision and how likely they are; drop caveats that would not change it.\n5. Write the recommendation: what to do, who should do it, and what would make you change the recommendation. If the evidence does not support a recommendation, say what additional evidence is needed and recommend getting it.\n6. Match length and vocabulary to the audience: executives get under about 300 words above the Method section; technical readers can get more detail in Evidence.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only numbers that appear in the results or are simple arithmetic on them (show the arithmetic in Method). Never invent figures, benchmarks or quotes.\n- Match the strength of language to the evidence: \"caused\" only for experiments or strong designs; otherwise \"is associated with\" or \"coincided with\".\n- If the results contradict each other or are too thin to support any headline, say so and list what is missing instead of writing a confident report.\n- No jargon without a short gloss. No filler phrases (\"it is important to note\").\n- Round sensibly (two significant figures for most business numbers) and keep units and periods on every figure.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Headline\nOne or two sentences: the finding and what it means for the decision.\n\n## Recommendation\nWhat to do, owner, and the condition that would change it.\n\n## Evidence\nTwo to four short paragraphs or bullets, each a supporting point with its number in context. Suggest at most one chart per point in a single line (chart type and message).\n\n## Caveats\nBullets, only those that could change the decision.\n\n## Next steps\nNumbered actions with owners if known.\n\n## Method\nTwo to four lines: data, period, approach, and any arithmetic you did.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "check-analysis-for-pitfalls",
          "write-weekly-metrics-update"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "data-analyst"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:d9f817335a61bc00fb627068c871318be50e42f20a9bb307b240d1aab69f2fbe"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-kubernetes-manifests",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write Kubernetes manifests",
      "description": "Writes production-ready Kubernetes manifests for a service with probes, resource requests, a disruption budget and a restricted security context. Use when deploying a service to a cluster.",
      "category": "devops",
      "tags": [
        "probes",
        "pod-security",
        "resource-limits",
        "kustomize"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "service",
          "description": "The service to deploy - image, ports, protocol, dependencies, config and secrets it reads, expected traffic, statefulness.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "environment",
          "description": "Environment the manifests target; sets replica counts, budgets and sizing.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "dev",
            "staging",
            "prod"
          ],
          "default": "prod"
        },
        {
          "name": "packaging",
          "description": "How to package the output.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "plain",
            "kustomize",
            "helm"
          ],
          "default": "plain"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nMost Kubernetes outages caused by manifests come from a short list: liveness probes that check a database and restart every pod when it blips, no readiness probe so traffic hits pods that are still starting, missing memory requests so the scheduler overpacks nodes, a disruption budget that blocks every node drain, all replicas on one node or zone, and containers running as root with a writable filesystem. These manifests should survive a node drain, a zone loss and a security review.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite Kubernetes manifests for this service, for the {{environment}} environment, packaged as {{packaging}}:\n{{service}}\n\n1. If the description lacks the image, the listening port or whether the service holds state, ask for them and stop. Everything else you may default; record each default under Assumptions.\n2. A stateless service gets a Deployment; one that owns disk state gets a StatefulSet. Say which and why.\n3. Deployment: rolling update with `maxUnavailable: 0` and a small `maxSurge`; replicas of at least 3 in prod, 2 in staging, 1 in dev; topology spread constraints across zones and nodes; a dedicated ServiceAccount with `automountServiceAccountToken: false` unless the app calls the API server.\n4. Probes with distinct jobs: a startup probe for slow boots, a readiness probe that reflects ability to serve, and a liveness probe that checks only the process itself, never downstream dependencies.\n5. Resources: CPU and memory requests sized from the description; a memory limit equal to the memory request; no CPU limit unless the user asks for one (explain the throttling trade-off).\n6. Security context: `runAsNonRoot`, a numeric non-zero UID, `readOnlyRootFilesystem` (with an `emptyDir` for any scratch path), `allowPrivilegeEscalation: false`, all capabilities dropped, `seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault`. Label the namespace for the `restricted` Pod Security Standard.\n7. Graceful shutdown: a `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` and a short `preStop` sleep so endpoints are removed before the process stops.\n8. Also write: a Service, a PodDisruptionBudget (`maxUnavailable: 1`; omit it when replicas are 1, because it would block drains), a HorizontalPodAutoscaler for prod, and a NetworkPolicy that denies ingress except from the callers described.\n9. Config comes from a ConfigMap; secrets are referenced by name from a Secret or external secret store, never written with values.\n10. Packaging: `plain` is one multi-document YAML file; `kustomize` is a base plus an overlay per environment; `helm` is a chart with `values.yaml`, templates and per-environment values files.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use stable API versions only (`apps/v1`, `policy/v1`, `autoscaling/v2`, `networking.k8s.io/v1`).\n- Pin the image by digest or an immutable version tag, never `latest`.\n- Do not invent hostnames, registry paths or secret names; use clearly marked placeholders such as `REPLACE_ME_REGISTRY` and list them under Assumptions.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\nBullets: every default and placeholder.\n## Manifests\nOne fenced `yaml` block per file, headed by its path.\n## Why these values\nA table: setting, value, reason. Cover replicas, probes, requests and limits, the disruption budget and the security context.\n## Verify\nCommands: `kubectl apply --dry-run=server`, a schema check such as kubeconform, and how to confirm the rollout and a node drain behave as intended.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "slim-container-image"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:5d2fb67a932f11c4652cae5c0f1c2ccc1fd62c2ea8a25f3ca51779b96011eca2"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-learning-objectives",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write measurable learning objectives",
      "description": "Writes measurable learning objectives with Bloom verbs, conditions and criteria, each aligned to an assessment method that would show mastery. Use when planning a lesson, module or course.",
      "category": "course-design",
      "tags": [
        "blooms-taxonomy",
        "constructive-alignment",
        "outcomes"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "topic",
          "description": "The content or unit the objectives cover, optionally with the goal behind it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Optional audience and level, e.g. \"Grade 6\", \"nursing students in year 2\", \"new sales hires\".",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "count",
          "description": "Number of objectives.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 5
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nAn objective is useful only if you could watch a learner and decide whether they have met it. \"Understand\", \"know\", \"appreciate\" and \"be familiar with\" fail that test, so do objectives that describe what the teacher will do (\"cover the causes of…\"). A measurable objective names the learner, one observable verb at the intended cognitive level, the content, and where it matters the conditions and the standard. It also points straight to how it will be assessed.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite {{count}} learning objectives for the topic below{{#audience}}, for {{audience}}{{/audience}}.\n\n<topic>\n{{topic}}\n</topic>\n\n1. Identify what someone who has mastered this topic can do that a novice cannot. Use that as the source of the objectives, not the list of content.\n2. Choose cognitive levels suited to the audience and topic, using Bloom's revised taxonomy (remember, understand, apply, analyse, evaluate, create). Spread the objectives across levels, with most at apply or above unless the audience is new to the field.\n3. Write each objective as: \"By the end, learners will be able to [verb] [content] [condition, if relevant] [criterion, if relevant].\"\n   - One verb per objective. No \"understand\", \"know\", \"learn\", \"appreciate\", \"be aware of\".\n   - Use a verb that matches the level and can be observed: identify, explain, calculate, compare, diagnose, justify, design, critique.\n   - Add a condition (\"given a patient case\", \"using a calculator\") or criterion (\"with no more than one error\", \"within 10 minutes\") when it changes what mastery means.\n4. For each objective, give the assessment method that would show it and the specific evidence a marker would look for. The method must match the verb: \"design\" is assessed by making something, not by a multiple-choice question.\n5. Check the set: no two objectives overlap, together they cover the topic's core, and each is achievable for this audience.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- If the topic is too vague to write measurable objectives for, ask up to two questions about the goal and the audience, then stop.\n- Keep the topic's terminology, and keep objectives to one sentence each.\n- If {{count}} is too many for a narrow topic, write fewer and say so instead of padding with trivial recall objectives.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Objectives\nA table: # | Objective | Bloom level | Assessment method | Evidence of mastery.\n## Notes\nUp to 3 bullets: coverage gaps, assumptions about the audience, and any objective that needs a resource or condition the teacher should confirm.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "design-course-outline",
          "create-rubric"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:40050b1889534371d27c959de911f856490bb2d8485890ce03a1a3121a6cfc1f"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-lesson-plan",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a lesson plan",
      "description": "Writes a lesson plan with measurable objectives, timed activities, checks for understanding, differentiation, materials and an exit ticket. Use when planning a single lesson.",
      "category": "teaching",
      "tags": [
        "lesson-design",
        "exit-ticket",
        "formative-assessment"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "topic",
          "description": "What the lesson teaches, e.g. \"comparing fractions with unlike denominators\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "grade_level",
          "description": "Grade, age or course, e.g. \"Grade 5\", \"Year 9\", \"first-year undergraduate\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "duration_minutes",
          "description": "Lesson length in minutes.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 50
        },
        {
          "name": "objectives",
          "description": "Optional objectives or curriculum standards the lesson must meet. Without them, objectives are drafted.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA lesson plan earns its keep in the classroom, not on paper. Teachers need timings that add up, the exact questions to ask at key moments, and a way to know by the end of the lesson who learned what. Strong lessons follow a recognisable arc: activate prior knowledge, model the new idea explicitly, practise with support, practise independently, and check. Checks for understanding happen throughout, not only at the end.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{duration_minutes}}-minute lesson on **{{topic}}** for **{{grade_level}}**.\n{{#objectives}}\nObjectives or standards to meet:\n<objectives>\n{{objectives}}\n</objectives>\n{{/objectives}}\n\n1. Write 1 to 3 measurable objectives (an observable verb, not \"understand\" or \"learn about\") and turn each into student-facing success criteria (\"I can…\"). If objectives were given, keep their intent and make them measurable.\n2. Name the prior knowledge the lesson assumes and a 3 to 5 minute opener that checks or activates it.\n3. Sequence the lesson with timings that sum exactly to {{duration_minutes}} minutes:\n   - Opener / do-now\n   - Explicit teaching and modelling (I do), with a worked example written out\n   - Guided practice (we do), with the questions the teacher asks\n   - Independent or collaborative practice (you do)\n   - Exit ticket and closure\n   Adjust the proportions to the age group and topic, but keep the teacher talk in any single block to about 10 to 15 minutes.\n4. Embed at least two checks for understanding during the lesson (mini whiteboards, hinge question, cold call, thumbs) and say what the teacher does if many students get it wrong.\n5. Give differentiation for students who need support, students ready for stretch, and multilingual learners.\n6. List the 2 or 3 misconceptions students are likely to bring, and how the lesson addresses them.\n7. Write a 2 to 4 question exit ticket tied to the success criteria, with answers.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Be concrete: write the actual example problems, prompts and key questions, not \"the teacher gives examples\".\n- Keep the content accurate and age-appropriate. If the topic is contested or sensitive for this age, note how to handle it.\n- If a fact you need is missing (the curriculum, the class's prior unit, available technology), make a reasonable assumption and list it under Overview rather than stopping to ask.\n- Use only materials a typical classroom has, unless the teacher listed others.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Overview\nTopic, grade, duration, and any assumptions.\n## Objectives and success criteria\nObjectives, then \"I can…\" statements.\n## Materials\nBullets.\n## Lesson sequence\nA table: Time (minutes) | Phase | Teacher does | Students do | Check for understanding. The time column adds to {{duration_minutes}}. Put the worked example and key questions below the table.\n## Differentiation\nSupport · Stretch · Multilingual learners, a few bullets each.\n## Anticipated misconceptions\nMisconception → how the lesson addresses it.\n## Exit ticket\nNumbered questions with answers.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "differentiate-lesson",
          "design-classroom-activity",
          "create-rubric"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "instructional-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:56389f6be9c4c2f6527ddcb6b11d752602fbe60a4263fd5c9ddf5ce648ad22e8"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-literature-review-section",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a literature review section",
      "description": "Synthesises sources into a thematic literature review section with in-text citations and a reference list, organised by idea rather than paper by paper. Use for theses, articles and proposals.",
      "category": "literature-review",
      "tags": [
        "synthesis",
        "citations",
        "thesis"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "sources",
          "description": "The sources to synthesise, each with full citation details and its abstract, notes or matrix row.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "research_question",
          "description": "The question or argument the section has to set up.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "citation_style",
          "description": "Citation style for in-text citations and the reference list.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "apa",
            "mla",
            "chicago",
            "harvard",
            "ieee",
            "vancouver"
          ],
          "default": "apa"
        },
        {
          "name": "word_limit",
          "description": "Maximum length of the section in words, excluding references.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 1200
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nThe most common weakness in literature reviews is the \"annotated bibliography\": one paragraph per paper, each starting with an author's name. Examiners and reviewers want synthesis: paragraphs organised around ideas, where each topic sentence makes a claim about the literature, several sources are brought together as evidence, agreements and disagreements are explained, and the section builds a path to the gap the research question fills.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a literature review section of at most {{word_limit}} words that sets up this research question:\n<research_question>\n{{research_question}}\n</research_question>\n\nUsing only these sources:\n<sources>\n{{sources}}\n</sources>\n\n1. Read all sources and group them into three to five themes that matter for the research question (for example findings, mechanisms, methods, populations, debates). A source can serve several themes.\n2. Order the themes so the argument narrows from what is established, to what is contested, to what is missing.\n3. Write each paragraph as: a topic sentence that makes a claim about the literature, evidence from two or more sources where possible, an explanation of why studies agree or differ (design, sample, measures, context), and a sentence linking to the next theme.\n4. Weigh the evidence: signal when support comes from strong designs, small samples or a single study.\n5. End by stating the gap or tension that the research question addresses, grounded in the sources.\n6. Cite in {{citation_style}} style and build the reference list from the details provided.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Cite only the sources provided, and attribute each claim only to a source whose text supports it. Never add a source from memory.\n- If a citation detail is missing (year, pages, DOI, journal), keep the gap visible as \"[missing: year]\" rather than guessing it.\n- Do not start paragraphs with author names or \"Study X found\". Lead with the idea.\n- Report the strength of findings faithfully; do not turn \"associated with\" into \"causes\" or one study into \"research shows\".\n- Stay within the word limit, and write in formal academic prose in the third person unless the sources show the field uses something else.\n- If the sources do not bear on the research question, or are too few to synthesise (fewer than about four), say so first and write the best section possible, marked as a draft.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nThe section itself, with a short heading and optional theme subheadings, then:\n## Synthesis map\nA table: theme | sources (in-text citations) | the claim the section makes about them.\n## References\nThe reference list in {{citation_style}} style, with \"[missing: …]\" markers where details were not provided.\n## Check before submitting\nThe word count, plus bullets listing every \"[missing: …]\" marker and any claim that rests on a single source.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "build-literature-matrix",
          "find-research-gaps"
        ],
        "workflows": [
          "literature-review-track"
        ],
        "rules": [
          "source-citation-rules"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:f4debfff3e6312dc28046267aa72b263c9a1159e94a31caf41910567d5813bc2"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-llm-eval-suite",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an eval suite for an LLM feature",
      "description": "Writes an eval set for an LLM feature with golden, edge and adversarial cases, graders matched to each criterion, and pass thresholds. Use before shipping or changing a model, prompt or pipeline.",
      "category": "ai-ml",
      "tags": [
        "evals",
        "llm-as-judge",
        "prompt-injection",
        "golden-set"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify",
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "feature",
          "description": "What the LLM feature does, who uses it, what a good output looks like, and what it must never do.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "sample_inputs",
          "description": "Real or realistic inputs the feature receives, with good outputs if you have them. Remove personal data first.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "grader",
          "description": "Grading approach to use. mixed picks the cheapest reliable grader for each criterion.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "exact",
            "rubric",
            "model-judge",
            "mixed"
          ],
          "default": "mixed"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nAn eval suite is the executable spec of an LLM feature. Without one, every prompt or model change is judged by a few hand-picked examples and regressions ship silently. Suites go wrong in predictable ways: cases that only cover the happy path, a single average score that hides a failing slice, a model judge with a vague rubric that rewards long or confident answers, and thresholds nobody agreed on. Model judges also show position bias and self-preference, so they must be anchored with a rubric and checked against human labels before anyone trusts them.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite an eval suite for this feature:\n{{feature}}\n{{#sample_inputs}}\n\nSample inputs:\n{{sample_inputs}}\n{{/sample_inputs}}\n\nGrading approach: {{grader}}.\n\n1. Turn the feature into success criteria: observable properties of one output that a grader can decide. Mark each as a hard requirement (must hold on every case, such as valid JSON, no leaked system prompt, refusal of out-of-scope requests) or a quality criterion (scored). If the description does not say what a good output is, ask before writing cases.\n2. Write 20 to 40 cases, each tagged with a slice:\n   - golden (about 60%): typical inputs, built from the samples when given;\n   - edge: empty or minimal input, very long input, mixed languages, ambiguous requests, unusual formatting, boundary values;\n   - adversarial: prompt injection inside the user content, requests to reveal instructions, out-of-scope or disallowed requests that fit this feature, inputs designed to trigger the known failure modes.\n   Use invented data only. Give a reference output or the key facts the output must contain wherever one exists.\n3. Pick a grader for each criterion. Use exact match, regex or schema validation for deterministic properties. Use a rubric for qualities. For a model judge, write the judge prompt: the criterion, a 1-to-5 or pass/fail scale with an anchor example for each level, the reference answer when there is one, reasoning before the verdict, and, for pairwise comparisons, both orderings. Say how to calibrate the judge: 20 to 50 human-labelled cases and the agreement level required before it is trusted.\n4. If the grading approach is exact or rubric only, say which criteria it cannot grade reliably and what you would use instead.\n5. Set thresholds: hard requirements at 100%, a pass rate per quality criterion, a minimum per slice, the number of runs per case to absorb sampling variance, and the rule for comparing a candidate against the current version.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every case must test something a criterion names. Drop cases that duplicate another case's purpose.\n- Do not use real names, emails or customer data in cases.\n- Keep the judge prompt self-contained, so it runs without this conversation.\n- Thresholds are starting values. Say how to revise them after the first runs.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Success criteria\nTable: id | criterion | hard or quality | grader.\n\n## Cases\nOne fenced YAML block. Each case: `id`, `slice`, `input`, `reference` (or `must_include`), `criteria` (ids).\n\n## Graders\nThe deterministic checks, the rubric, and the full judge prompt in a fenced block, plus the calibration procedure.\n\n## Thresholds and gating\nPass rules per criterion and slice, runs per case, and when a change may ship.\n\n## Gaps\nWhat the suite does not cover yet and what data would close it.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "design-rag-pipeline",
          "plan-fine-tuning"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "ml-engineer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:232ee4b36c12c2b5c39f9d073cfd362314901faae7cd3a40a69f099526d873f6"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-model-card",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a model card",
      "description": "Writes a model card with intended use, training data, metrics by slice, limitations and ethical considerations from training notes and eval results, flagging gaps. Use before releasing a model.",
      "category": "ai-ml",
      "tags": [
        "model-card",
        "responsible-ai",
        "fairness"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "model_notes",
          "description": "Training notes, configuration, data description, intended use and anything known about limitations.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "eval_results",
          "description": "Evaluation results, ideally broken down by slice (language, region, device, demographic group) with sample sizes.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA model card tells someone deciding whether to use a model what it is for, what it was trained and tested on, where it works and where it fails. Readers include engineers integrating it, reviewers approving its release and people affected by its decisions. Weak cards read like marketing: one headline metric, no slices, limitations that are generic or invented, and no out-of-scope uses. A useful card states only what the evidence supports and says plainly what was never measured.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a model card from these notes:\n{{model_notes}}\n{{#eval_results}}\n\nEvaluation results:\n{{eval_results}}\n{{/eval_results}}\n\n1. Fill each section from the evidence: model details (name, version, type, architecture or base model, date, owner, license), intended use and users, out-of-scope uses, training data (sources, size, time range, preprocessing, known gaps), evaluation data, metrics, limitations, ethical considerations, and recommendations for users.\n2. Derive out-of-scope uses from the evidence. For example, training data in one language makes other languages out of scope, and data from one period makes later periods unverified.\n3. Report metrics overall and by every slice available, with sample sizes and confidence intervals where they exist. Call out the largest gap between slices with its numbers.\n4. Where the notes say nothing, write \"Not documented\" and add a precise question to Gaps to fill naming who or what could answer it.\n5. Flag contradictions between the notes and the results, such as a claim of multilingual support with English-only evaluation.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent a number, dataset, license or limitation. Mark anything you inferred as an inference.\n- Do not round or average away a disparity between slices.\n- Write for a technical reader who is not on the team, in plain language, defining any metric name a reader may not know.\n- Keep marketing language out (\"state-of-the-art\", \"robust\", \"unbiased\").\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nA Markdown model card with these headings, in order: Model details, Intended use, Out-of-scope uses, Training data, Evaluation data, Metrics, Limitations, Ethical considerations, Recommendations, Gaps to fill. Present metrics as a table: slice | metric | value | sample size. Gaps to fill is a numbered list of questions.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "ml-engineer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:ff9c2bd768dd7db27e1e0906c4df7a7a79fcfb152015cdac72b7fc10736d26c8"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-onboarding-guide",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a developer onboarding guide",
      "description": "Writes an onboarding guide for a repository covering setup, an architecture map, first tasks and known gotchas, with every command checked against the repo. Use for new hires or contributors.",
      "category": "docs",
      "tags": [
        "developer-experience",
        "getting-started",
        "new-hire"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "repo",
          "description": "The repository to document (path or name), plus anything the code cannot tell you, such as team channels, access to request or the review process.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who the guide is for. new-hire = an employee with access to internal systems; contributor = an outside contributor with only the public repository.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "new-hire",
            "contributor"
          ],
          "default": "new-hire"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nOnboarding guides rot because they are written from memory: a setup step was changed in CI but not in the README, a required environment variable was never written down, and the architecture section describes the system as it was planned. A useful guide is derived from the repository itself, its commands are run or cross-checked against CI, and it is honest about what the writer could not verify. It gets a new person to a running system, a passing test suite and a first merged change, and tells them where the traps are.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite an onboarding guide for {{repo}}, for a {{audience}}.\n\n1. Read the sources of truth before writing: README and docs folder, manifests and lockfiles, version files (.nvmrc, .tool-versions, rust-toolchain and the like), Makefile or task runner, Dockerfile and compose files, environment templates (.env.example), CI workflows, contributing guide, code owners, and the top-level directory layout.\n2. Derive setup from what CI actually runs, not only from the README. Where they disagree, follow CI and note the discrepancy.\n3. If you can run commands, run the setup, build, test and lint commands in a clean state and record what happened. Do not run commands that deploy, push, migrate shared databases or spend money. If you cannot run them, mark each command \"not run\".\n4. Build the architecture map: entry points, main modules and what each owns, how a typical request or job flows through the code, where data is stored, and external services the code calls. Link to the files.\n5. Pick 3 to 5 first tasks that touch different areas and are small: a labelled good-first issue, a missing test, a docs gap you found. Say what each teaches.\n6. Collect gotchas from evidence: discrepancies you found, scripts with surprising side effects, required services or secrets, slow or flaky test suites, generated files that must not be edited, platform-specific steps.\n7. For a contributor, cover only what is possible with public access (fork, DCO or CLA, how to run CI locally). For a new hire, include placeholders for access requests and people to ask, written as `TODO(owner): …` rather than invented names or links.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every command in the guide must come from the repository or be one you ran. Do not invent scripts, environment variables, URLs, channels or people.\n- Keep it scannable: numbered setup steps, one command per code block, expected output where it helps the reader know it worked.\n- Write for someone smart who knows the language but not this codebase. Define internal terms on first use.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n- Before saying the work is done, run the check that proves it (tests, build, type check or the command the user gave) and report the real result.\n- If you could not run a check, say so plainly and say which one.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Guide\nThe guide in Markdown with these sections: Prerequisites (with versions), Setup, Run it, Tests and checks, Architecture map, How work flows (branches, reviews, CI, release), First tasks, Gotchas, Where to get help.\n## Verification log\nTable: Command | Ran? | Result. Then any README and CI discrepancies.\n## Open questions\nWhat the maintainers must fill in or confirm, as a checklist.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "technical-writer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:1beed15445f1d88f41379ff998c263a7cff6b3d7fc1650425a649965aac4ba1e"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-peer-review",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a peer review",
      "description": "Writes a constructive manuscript review with a summary, major and minor issues, methodological and reporting concerns, and a reasoned recommendation. Use when refereeing a paper.",
      "category": "peer-review",
      "tags": [
        "manuscript",
        "referee-report",
        "reporting-guidelines"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "manuscript",
          "description": "The manuscript text, including methods, results, tables and supplementary material you were sent.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "venue",
          "description": "The journal or conference and its article type, so the review matches its scope and standards.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "focus",
          "description": "Anything the editor asked you to concentrate on, or your own area of expertise, for example \"the statistical analysis\".",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nEditors value reviews that show the reviewer understood the paper, separate fatal problems from fixable ones, explain why each issue matters, and say what would resolve it. Authors value reviews that are specific and respectful. Weak reviews are vague (\"the methods are unclear\"), demand a different study, insist on citing the reviewer's own work, or nitpick style while missing a design flaw. Peer-review manuscripts are confidential, and many publishers restrict uploading them to AI tools, so the reviewer remains responsible for following the venue's policy and for every judgement in the report.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nReview this manuscript{{#venue}} submitted to {{venue}}{{/venue}}:\n<manuscript>\n{{manuscript}}\n</manuscript>\n{{#focus}}\nGive extra attention to: {{focus}}\n{{/focus}}\n\n1. Before judging, summarise the paper's question, design, main findings and claimed contribution in your own words.\n2. Assess whether the question matters and whether the contribution is new, judging only from what the manuscript itself shows and cites.\n3. Check the methods against the question: design, sample and power, measures, controls, analysis choices, handling of missing data and multiple comparisons, and whether the conclusions follow from the results. Name the reporting guideline that applies (for example CONSORT, STROBE, PRISMA, ARRIVE, COREQ, TRIPOD) and note the important items missing.\n4. Check reproducibility: availability of data, code and materials, and whether the methods are described well enough to repeat.\n5. Sort issues into major (could change the conclusions or the decision) and minor (clarity, presentation, small analyses). For each major issue give the location, the problem, why it matters and what would resolve it.\n6. Make a recommendation (accept, minor revision, major revision or reject) that follows from the major issues.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every issue points to a specific section, table, figure or line and proposes a resolution. No vague criticism.\n- Stay within the paper's aims: do not ask for a different study, and request new experiments or data only when the conclusions cannot stand without them.\n- Do not ask the authors to cite particular papers unless a missing citation is needed for a specific claim, and never suggest references you cannot verify.\n- Be respectful and impersonal: critique the work, never the authors. Acknowledge real strengths.\n- Do not speculate about the authors' identity or intentions. Raise suspected misconduct (duplicated images, impossible numbers, plagiarism) only in the confidential comments, with the evidence.\n- If the text provided is only part of the manuscript, say so and limit the review to what you can see.\n- Start the report with one line reminding the reviewer to check that the venue permits AI assistance and to keep the manuscript confidential.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nOne reminder line, then:\n## Summary\nOne paragraph.\n## Overall assessment\nStrengths and the main concerns in three to five sentences.\n## Major issues\nNumbered: location — problem — why it matters — what would resolve it.\n## Minor issues\nNumbered, one or two lines each.\n## Reporting and reproducibility\nThe guideline that applies, missing items, and data and code availability.\n## Recommendation\nThe recommendation and the two or three reasons that decide it.\n## Confidential comments to the editor\nAnything not for the authors, or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "respond-to-reviewers"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "research-methodologist",
          "statistician"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:b83f27e4e558f219b24edc696933385058f35d5a3c6078c51c78cd8c0b5f0f32"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-plotting-code",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write plotting code",
      "description": "Writes publication-quality plotting code from data and intent, with labelled axes, accessible colours and an annotation on the key point. Use for matplotlib, seaborn, plotly, ggplot2 or Vega-Lite.",
      "category": "data-visualization",
      "tags": [
        "matplotlib",
        "ggplot2",
        "annotation",
        "colour-blind-safe"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "data",
          "description": "The data (a small table pasted inline) or its structure (file or dataframe name, columns and types, a few sample rows).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "intent",
          "description": "What the chart should show and the key point to annotate, plus the output target (slide, paper, web) and size if it matters.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "library",
          "description": "Plotting library to write the code for.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "matplotlib",
            "seaborn",
            "plotly",
            "ggplot2",
            "vega-lite"
          ],
          "default": "matplotlib"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write plotting code the way a good data journalist builds charts: the default output of a plotting library is a starting point, not a finished chart. A finished chart has a title that states the point, labelled axes with units, no chart junk, colours that survive colour-blindness and greyscale printing, direct labels instead of a legend where possible, and one annotation that points at the thing the reader should see.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite {{library}} code for this chart.\n\n<data>\n{{data}}\n</data>\n\n<intent>\n{{intent}}\n</intent>\n\n1. Choose the chart type that best serves the intent, in one sentence. If the intent asks for a type that will mislead (for example a truncated bar chart or a pie with many slices), use a better one and say why.\n2. Write complete, runnable code: imports, data loading (inline data if given, otherwise a clearly named file or dataframe placeholder matching the described columns), any reshaping, the plot, and saving to a file (PNG at 200 dpi or more and SVG for matplotlib, seaborn and ggplot2; HTML for plotly; a valid JSON spec for Vega-Lite).\n3. Apply these defaults unless the intent says otherwise:\n   - An action title stating the point, a subtitle with units and period, and a source or note line.\n   - Axis labels with units; thousands separators, percentages and dates formatted for reading.\n   - Bars starting at zero; sorted categories when order is not inherent.\n   - A colour-blind-safe palette (Okabe-Ito or viridis for sequential data); the key series in one strong colour and the rest in grey.\n   - Direct labels at line ends or on bars instead of a legend when there are five or fewer series.\n   - Minimal gridlines, no top and right spines, no 3D or shadows.\n   - One annotation (text plus an arrow or marker) at the key point named in the intent.\n4. Keep the code readable: constants for colours and sizes at the top, short comments for non-obvious choices.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the chosen library and its normal companions (pandas or numpy for Python libraries, the tidyverse and scales for ggplot2). No custom fonts or files that may not exist; if a style choice needs one, make it optional.\n- Do not invent data. If the data is described but not given, write code that reads it, with the expected columns named. If key columns needed for the intent are missing, ask for them and stop.\n- The annotation must be computed from the data where possible (for example the maximum, or the last point), not hard-coded coordinates, so the chart stays right when data updates.\n- Make the figure size suit the target: wide for slides, column width for papers, responsive for web.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Chart choice\nOne or two sentences.\n\n## Code\nOne complete code block.\n\n## Notes\nUp to four bullets: how to adapt it (other series to highlight, size for another target), and anything assumed about the data.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "choose-chart-type",
          "critique-chart"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:f2fb3f7fcd6f6eb4b2c4fd5b1339c365839210fd27e419e44c86d2e89a9cac3d"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-property-based-tests",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write property-based tests",
      "description": "Finds the invariants a function must keep and writes property-based tests with generators that shrink well. Use when example-based tests miss edge cases in parsers, encoders or pure logic.",
      "category": "testing",
      "tags": [
        "property-based-testing",
        "hypothesis",
        "fast-check",
        "proptest",
        "invariants"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "code",
          "description": "The function, module or file to test, pasted or as a path.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "framework",
          "description": "Property-testing library to use, for example Hypothesis, fast-check, proptest, jqwik or QuickCheck. Detected from the repo when left empty.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nProperty-based tests state a rule that must hold for every valid input and let a generator search for a counterexample, then shrink it to the smallest failing case. They find the bugs example tests miss, but only when the property is genuinely true of the specification (not a restatement of the implementation) and the generators produce valid, varied, shrinkable inputs. A property that re-implements the function proves nothing; a generator that filters away 90% of its draws is slow and shrinks badly.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite property-based tests for:\n{{code}}\n\nLibrary: {{#framework}}{{framework}}.{{/framework}} If no library is named, detect it from the project's manifests and existing tests (Hypothesis for Python, fast-check for JavaScript and TypeScript, proptest for Rust, jqwik for Java, gopter or the standard testing/quick for Go). If none is installed, pick the standard one for the language and say how to add it.\n\n1. Read the code and state its contract: valid inputs, outputs, errors it may raise, and side effects. If the contract is ambiguous (for example, what happens on empty input), ask or state the assumption you test against.\n2. Find candidate properties, preferring these patterns:\n   - round-trip: decode(encode(x)) == x, parse(print(x)) == x;\n   - invariants: output is sorted, length preserved, total conserved, no duplicates, within bounds;\n   - idempotence: f(f(x)) == f(x);\n   - oracle or model: agrees with a simpler, obviously correct implementation or an in-memory model of a stateful system;\n   - metamorphic: a known change to the input causes a predictable change to the output;\n   - algebraic: commutativity, associativity, identity elements where the domain promises them;\n   - robustness: never crashes or hangs on any input of the right type, and fails only with documented errors.\n   Keep only properties that follow from the contract. Discard any that just mirror the implementation.\n3. Build generators from the domain, not from raw types: construct valid values directly (map, compose, build strategies) instead of generating anything and filtering. Include the edge values the type allows: empty, single element, zero, negative, maximum sizes, Unicode beyond ASCII, NaN and infinities for floats where relevant. Bound sizes so a run stays fast.\n4. Write the tests in the project's style and test runner. Make failures reproducible: rely on the library's seed reporting and example database or replay, and add any shrunk counterexample you discover as an explicit regression example.\n5. If you can run the tests, do so and report the result. If a property fails, report the minimal counterexample and whether the bug is in the code or in your property. Do not change the code under test.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every property must name the contract clause it checks. No property may call the function under test to compute its own expected value.\n- Avoid filter or assume calls that reject more than a small fraction of draws; restructure the generator instead.\n- Keep default example counts unless there is a reason to change them, and say why if you do.\n- Do not fix bugs you find; report them.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n- Fix the behaviour, not the test. Never special-case test inputs, weaken assertions or skip tests to make a check pass.\n- If a test looks wrong, explain why and ask before changing it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Properties\nTable: Property | Pattern | Contract clause it checks.\n## Generators\nOne line per generator: what it builds and which edge values it covers.\n## Tests\nThe complete test file in one code block, with imports.\n## Counterexamples\nShrunk failing inputs with a one-line diagnosis each, or \"None found\" with the number of examples run. If you could not run the tests, say so.\n## How to run\nThe exact command, including how to replay a failure from its seed.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "test-engineer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:caa951298cacec62c0065ab1f2bad142f6049fb31629fd2186bfe2afa5ea5760"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-regex",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a regular expression",
      "description": "Builds a regular expression from plain-language intent and example strings, explains each part and lists the edge cases it accepts or rejects. Use when you need a tested pattern.",
      "category": "implementation",
      "tags": [
        "regex",
        "pattern-matching",
        "validation"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "intent",
          "description": "What the pattern should match, in plain words, and where it will be used (validation, search, extraction).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "should_match",
          "description": "Example strings that must match, one per line. Mark the part to capture if extracting.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "should_not_match",
          "description": "Example strings that must not match, one per line.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "flavor",
          "description": "Regex engine the pattern must run in.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "javascript",
            "python",
            "pcre",
            "go",
            "posix"
          ],
          "default": "javascript"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nRegexes look right and fail quietly. The common faults are a missing anchor that lets the pattern match inside a longer string, a feature the target engine does not support, a `$` that also matches before a trailing newline, nested quantifiers that backtrack catastrophically on hostile input, and a pattern that was never actually run against the examples it was built from.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{flavor}} regular expression for: {{intent}}\n\nMust match:\n{{should_match}}\n\nMust not match:\n{{should_not_match}}\n\n1. Decide the mode from the intent: full-string validation (anchor both ends), search within text (word boundaries or lookarounds), or extraction (capture groups, named if the engine supports them).\n2. Respect the engine:\n   - javascript: use the `u` flag for Unicode; `\\d` and `\\w` are ASCII-only.\n   - python: use `re.fullmatch` for validation, or `\\Z` rather than `$`; in Python 3, `\\d` and `\\w` match Unicode unless you pass `re.ASCII`.\n   - pcre: `$` matches before a final newline; use `\\z` for a strict end. Possessive quantifiers and atomic groups are available.\n   - go: RE2 has no lookaround and no backreferences. Rewrite the logic without them, or say that code must do that part.\n   - posix: ERE only. No `\\d`, lazy quantifiers or lookaround; use bracket expressions like `[0-9]` and `[[:alpha:]]`.\n3. Prefer the simplest pattern that passes every example. Avoid nested quantifiers over overlapping classes such as `(a+)+` or `(\\w|\\d)*`.\n4. Test it. Walk every example through the pattern and record the result. If a code tool is available, run them for real and say so. If any example fails, fix the pattern and repeat.\n5. Probe the edges the examples do not cover: empty string, leading and trailing whitespace, newlines, Unicode letters and digits, very long input, and near-misses of the valid shape.\n6. If the examples contradict the intent or each other, say which ones and which reading you followed.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never claim an example passes unless you checked it.\n- If a regex is the wrong tool (nested structures, full email RFC compliance, real date validity such as 31 February, HTML), say so in one sentence, give the pragmatic pattern anyway, and name what code must check.\n- Show the pattern both as a literal and as an escaped string for the language when they differ.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Pattern\nA code block with the pattern and flags, then one line on the matching mode.\n\n## How it works\n| Part | Meaning |\n\n## Test results\n| Input | Expected | Result |\nEvery given example, then the edge cases you added.\n\n## Edge cases\nInputs it accepts that someone might not expect, and inputs it rejects that might be valid. One line each.\n\n## Usage\nA 3 to 6 line snippet in the language of the chosen flavor (shell `grep -E` for posix).\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nIntent: a hex colour in CSS, full-string. Should match: `#fff`, `#A1B2C3`. Should not match: `fff`, `#abcd`, `#12345g`. Flavor: javascript.\n\n## Pattern\n```\n/^#(?:[0-9a-f]{3}|[0-9a-f]{6})$/i\n```\nFull-string validation.\n\n## Edge cases\n- Rejects 4- and 8-digit forms with alpha (`#abcd`, `#11223344`), which CSS Color Level 4 allows. Add `|[0-9a-f]{4}|[0-9a-f]{8}` if you need them.\n</example>\n</examples>",
      "hash": "sha256:c9692660c83f47178f7bfe6d7bb6309f90d047cbb8c502cfd24dbc95736be6de"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-release-notes",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write release notes",
      "description": "Turns merged pull requests or commits into release notes for a chosen audience, grouped by impact and written as outcomes without internal jargon. Use when shipping a version.",
      "category": "docs",
      "tags": [
        "release-notes",
        "changelog",
        "breaking-changes"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "changes",
          "description": "Merged PR titles and descriptions, commit messages, or a compare log for the release.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who reads the notes. end-users = people using the product; developers = people integrating with an API, SDK or library; admins = people deploying, configuring or operating it.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "end-users",
            "developers",
            "admins"
          ],
          "default": "end-users"
        },
        {
          "name": "version",
          "description": "Version number or release name for the heading.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nCommit logs describe what engineers did; release notes describe what changed for the reader. Readers scan for three things: does anything break or need action from me, what can I now do that I could not before, and was the problem I reported fixed. Notes that list refactors, ticket numbers and component names bury those answers. Notes that inflate a minor fix or guess at a change's effect mislead people.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite release notes{{#version}} for {{version}}{{/version}} for {{audience}} from these changes:\n{{changes}}\n\n1. Classify every change: breaking or action required, new, improved, fixed, security, deprecated, or internal (no effect the reader can notice).\n2. Drop internal changes: refactors, CI, test, tooling and dependency bumps, unless they change behaviour, performance the reader would notice, supported versions, or fix a security issue.\n3. Merge changes that are parts of one outcome into a single item.\n4. Rewrite each item as one sentence about the outcome for the reader, in their words: \"You can now export invoices as PDF\" rather than \"Add PdfRenderer to InvoiceService\". Fixes say what used to go wrong. For developers, name the public API, endpoint, flag or config key affected, and nothing more internal than that. For admins, include configuration, migration, permission, compatibility and deployment impact.\n5. Every breaking change or required action gets what breaks, who is affected and the exact step to take, before anything else.\n6. When a change's user-facing effect is unclear from the input, do not guess: put it under \"Questions\".\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- No internal details: no class, file or component names, ticket numbers, author names or architecture terms, except public API names for developers.\n- Do not overstate: no \"blazing fast\", \"major overhaul\" or invented numbers. Use a performance figure only if the input gives it.\n- Keep each item to one sentence. Order sections by impact on the reader, and items within a section by how many readers they affect.\n- Omit empty sections.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Release notes\nThe notes, ready to paste: a heading with the version if given, an optional one-sentence highlight, then sections in this order: Action required, New, Improved, Fixed, Security, Deprecated.\n## Left out\nBullets: each dropped change and why it was left out (internal, merged into another item).\n## Questions\nBullets: changes whose user-facing effect you could not determine. Or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "hash": "sha256:9b89b801345fcd018067af471e6fd9e57571181d2cce0a4bae7460fc01194124"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-report-card-comments",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write report card comments",
      "description": "Writes specific, balanced report-card comments from a teacher's notes, with a strength, a next step and a consistent tone and length across a class. Use at reporting time.",
      "category": "teaching",
      "tags": [
        "report-cards",
        "parent-communication",
        "strengths-and-next-steps"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "student_notes",
          "description": "Notes per student, one block each, starting with the name and pronouns, e.g. \"Sam (he): strong reader, rushes maths, kind to peers, missed 2 homework\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "tone",
          "description": "formal for school-standard reporting language, warm for a friendlier register.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "formal",
            "warm"
          ],
          "default": "warm"
        },
        {
          "name": "max_words",
          "description": "Maximum words per comment.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 80
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nFamilies read report comments closely, often several times. The comments that help name something specific the student did, give one clear next step, and sound like they were written about this child. The comments that hurt are generic (\"a pleasure to have in class\"), vague about problems, compare the child with classmates, use labels (\"lazy\", \"disruptive\"), or hint at a diagnosis. Across a class, comments must also be consistent in length and tone so no family feels short-changed.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite report-card comments in a `{{tone}}` tone, at most {{max_words}} words each, from these notes:\n\n<student_notes>\n{{student_notes}}\n</student_notes>\n\nFor each student:\n1. Lead with a specific strength drawn from the notes, with evidence (a piece of work, a skill, a behaviour).\n2. Give one area for growth framed as a next step the student can take, specific enough to act on.\n3. Where it fits, add one way the family can support at home.\n4. Close on a forward-looking sentence.\n5. Use the student's name and the pronouns given. If no pronouns are given, use the name and \"they\".\n\nAcross the class:\n6. Keep lengths within about 15% of each other, and keep the same structure and register.\n7. Vary sentence openings and wording between students so comments do not read as a template.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only what is in the notes. Do not invent achievements, grades or incidents. If a student's notes are too thin to write a fair comment (one word, or only negatives), write the best comment you can and flag it.\n- Describe behaviour, not character: \"often begins tasks after reminders\" rather than \"lazy\".\n- No comparisons with other students, no medical or diagnostic language, no mention of family circumstances, discipline records or attendance unless the notes explicitly ask for it to be included.\n- Avoid jargon families will not know, and clichés (\"a joy to teach\", \"needs to apply themselves\").\n- `formal`: third person, school reporting register. `warm`: third person, friendly and encouraging, still professional.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Comments\nFor each student: a heading with the name, the comment as one paragraph, then \"(n words)\".\n## Check before sending\nBullets: students whose notes were too thin, anything you left out because it was sensitive, and any statement the teacher should verify.\n</output_format>",
      "hash": "sha256:651f5ceffbb7c6aa67a0a3abd86d6ea4d882927a98a9d46dc4d3ad8962e370ab"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-research-proposal",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a research proposal",
      "description": "Writes a research or grant proposal section (aims, significance, approach, timeline) mapped to the funder's criteria, with placeholders instead of invented data. Use for grant or thesis proposals.",
      "category": "scientific-writing",
      "tags": [
        "grant-writing",
        "specific-aims",
        "funding"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "project",
          "description": "Your project notes: the problem, the question, your approach, preliminary results, team and resources. More detail gives a stronger draft.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "funder_criteria",
          "description": "The funder's call text, review criteria, page or word limits and required headings, pasted as given.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "section",
          "description": "Which part to write.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "aims",
            "significance",
            "approach",
            "full"
          ],
          "default": "full"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nReviewers score proposals against published criteria, often in a few minutes per section. Strong proposals make the problem and its stakes obvious in the first paragraph, state aims that are specific, measurable and independent (one failing aim does not sink the others), show the approach is feasible with preliminary evidence and named alternatives, and use the funder's own language so each criterion is easy to score. Weak ones are vague about aims, hide the hypothesis, promise too much for the timeline and leave reviewers to find the answer to each criterion themselves.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite the \"{{section}}\" section of a proposal for this project:\n<project>\n{{project}}\n</project>\n{{#funder_criteria}}\nFunder call and review criteria:\n<criteria>\n{{funder_criteria}}\n</criteria>\n{{/funder_criteria}}\n\n1. Extract the review criteria, limits and required headings from the funder text. Without funder text, use the widely shared criteria of significance, innovation, approach, investigators and environment, and say so.\n2. Write the requested part:\n   - aims: an opening paragraph on the problem and the gap, the long-term goal, the objective of this proposal, the central hypothesis and its rationale, then two to four aims, each with a working hypothesis or objective, a one-line approach and the expected outcome, and a closing paragraph on the payoff.\n   - significance: the problem's size and consequences, what is known and the specific gap, and what will change if the aims succeed, for the field and for people.\n   - approach: per aim, the rationale, the design and methods, the analysis, expected results, potential problems with alternative strategies, and success benchmarks; then a timeline table by quarter or semester with milestones.\n   - full: all of the above in the funder's order, within its limits.\n3. Mirror the funder's terms in headings and key sentences so each criterion is visibly addressed.\n4. Check feasibility: flag aims that the timeline, team or budget in the notes cannot plausibly deliver.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts from the project notes and funder text. For anything missing (preliminary data, figures, citations, budget, collaborators' names), insert a visible placeholder such as \"[PRELIMINARY DATA: pilot n and effect]\" or \"[CITATION: prevalence of X]\". Never invent results, numbers or references.\n- Respect the funder's limits; if none are given, keep aims to about one page (roughly 500 words) and say what length you assumed for other sections.\n- Write in active voice with concrete verbs, and make each aim's success testable.\n- Do not overpromise: impact claims must follow from the aims.\n- If the project notes are too thin to write a credible section (for example no question or method), ask for the missing pieces first, listing exactly what is needed.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nThe requested section(s) under the funder's headings, then:\n## Criteria coverage\nA table: criterion | where it is addressed | strength (strong / adequate / weak) | how to strengthen.\n## Placeholders to fill\nA checklist of every placeholder in the draft.\n## Reviewer risks\nThree to five likely reviewer objections, each with the sentence or evidence that would pre-empt it.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "design-research-study",
          "find-research-gaps"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:d053695c9c9e28aede2ff6af228c1dc77f965864e15bbacb97d3f1f7781267a4"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-runbook",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an operational runbook",
      "description": "Writes a runbook for an alert or routine procedure with symptoms, diagnosis commands, ordered mitigations, verification and escalation. Use so on-call engineers can act without tribal knowledge.",
      "category": "incident",
      "tags": [
        "runbook",
        "alerting",
        "mitigation",
        "escalation"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "operate"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "alert_or_procedure",
          "description": "The alert (name, condition, query, threshold) or the routine procedure to document, e.g. \"rotate the TLS certificate on the public load balancer\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "system_context",
          "description": "Architecture, dashboards, log locations, tooling, owners and known past causes.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA runbook is read by a tired engineer who may never have touched this system, often in the middle of the night. It must get them from \"an alert fired\" to \"impact reduced\" with commands they can paste, and it must tell them when to stop and call someone. Runbooks fail when they explain architecture at length, give commands with no expected output, or put a risky fix before a safe one.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a runbook for:\n{{alert_or_procedure}}\n{{#system_context}}\nSystem context:\n{{system_context}}\n{{/system_context}}\n\n1. Decide which kind this is. For an alert, write the alert flow below. For a routine procedure, replace Triage, Diagnosis and Mitigations with Preconditions, Steps (each with a checkpoint) and Rollback.\n2. Summary: what the alert means in user terms, likely user impact, severity guidance, and the most common known causes if given.\n3. Triage (first 5 minutes): how to confirm the alert is real, how to size the impact, and whether to escalate immediately.\n4. Diagnosis: read-only checks in order of likelihood. Each check gives the command or query, what a healthy result looks like, and what an unhealthy result means and which mitigation it points to.\n5. Mitigations: ordered from safest and most reversible to riskiest. Each states when to use it, the exact steps, the risk, and how to undo it.\n6. Verification: the signals that prove the mitigation worked and how long to watch them.\n7. Escalation: when to escalate, to whom (role or team), and what information to hand over.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent hostnames, dashboard links, metric names, namespaces or team names. Use placeholders in angle brackets such as `<service-namespace>` and list every one under \"Fill before publishing\".\n- Put every command in a fenced block. Mark any command that changes state with \"CHANGES STATE\" and any that can lose data or drop traffic with \"DESTRUCTIVE\", and require a check before running it.\n- Keep it scannable: numbered steps, short sentences, no history lessons.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Summary\n## Triage\n## Diagnosis\n## Mitigations\n## Verification\n## Escalation\n## Fill before publishing\nA checklist of every placeholder and unconfirmed assumption.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "incident-commander"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:048cdfaffd6cbb88ae91af90082886744425e238b27a5e663eee77c2465cf9ca"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-screen-reader-test-plan",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a screen-reader test plan",
      "description": "Produces a manual screen-reader test script for a user flow on NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver or TalkBack, with the keystrokes or gestures and expected announcements for each step.",
      "category": "accessibility",
      "tags": [
        "screen-reader",
        "manual-testing",
        "assistive-technology"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "flow",
          "description": "The user flow to test, step by step, with the screens, controls and any dynamic updates (loading, errors, toasts).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "screen_readers",
          "description": "Screen readers to cover. Ones that do not exist on the chosen platform are dropped, with a note.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "NVDA, VoiceOver, TalkBack"
        },
        {
          "name": "platform",
          "description": "Where the flow runs.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "web",
            "ios",
            "android"
          ],
          "default": "web"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nTesters new to screen readers tend to Tab through a page and call it done. Real users navigate by headings, landmarks, form fields and lists. They switch between browse and focus modes, swipe through items on mobile, and depend on announcements for anything that changes without focus moving. Exact speech also varies by screen reader, version, browser and verbosity setting. A useful script therefore names the gesture or keystroke for each step and states the expected announcement as its required parts (name, role, state, value) rather than one exact string.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a manual screen-reader test script for this {{platform}} flow:\n\n{{flow}}\n\nScreen readers requested: {{screen_readers}}.\n\n1. Build the test matrix. Pair each screen reader with the browser or platform it is mainly used with: NVDA with Firefox or Chrome on Windows, JAWS with Chrome or Edge, VoiceOver with Safari on macOS, VoiceOver on iOS with Safari or the app, TalkBack with Chrome or the app on Android. Drop any that do not run on {{platform}} and say so.\n2. Write the setup: the versions to record, default verbosity, the speech viewer or log to turn on (NVDA Speech Viewer, VoiceOver caption panel, TalkBack's developer setting for speech output), and resetting state between runs.\n3. Start with orientation checks before the flow: the page or screen title is announced, the headings outline makes sense (H key or the rotor), landmarks are present and labelled, and the language is announced correctly.\n4. For each step of the flow, write:\n   - the action in each screen reader's own terms: NVDA and JAWS keys (H, D or R for landmarks, F for form fields, Tab, Enter, Space, Insert+F7 or Insert+F6 lists), VoiceOver keys (VO+Right Arrow, VO+Space, the rotor) or gestures (swipe right, double-tap, the rotor), and TalkBack gestures (swipe right, double-tap, reading controls);\n   - the expected announcement as name, role, state and value, for example \"Email, edit text, required, invalid entry\";\n   - the dynamic behaviour to confirm, such as where focus lands after a dialog opens or closes, a live-region announcement for async results, errors announced and linked to their field, and a loading state that is announced and then cleared;\n   - the pass criterion and the WCAG success criterion it maps to.\n5. Add negative checks: every control is reachable with the screen reader's standard navigation, not only by mouse or by touch exploration, decorative images are silent, and hidden content is not read out.\n6. If the flow description leaves out what happens at a step (validation, a success message, a redirect), list it under Coverage gaps instead of inventing behaviour.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not claim an exact announcement string unless the flow specifies the label text. Expected speech is the components, in any order the screen reader uses.\n- Keystrokes must be real for the named screen reader. If unsure of one, say so rather than guess.\n- Keep each step to one action, so a failure points to one place.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Setup\n| Screen reader | Browser or app | Platform | Settings to record |\nThen the setup and reset steps.\n\n## Test script\nFor each step:\n| Step | Action (per screen reader) | Expected announcement | Also check | Pass criterion | WCAG SC |\nOrientation checks come first.\n\n## Defect template\nFields to fill for a failure: step, screen reader and version, browser, actual speech (copied from the log), expected, and severity.\n\n## Coverage gaps\nUnspecified behaviours and parts of the flow not covered.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "accessibility-specialist"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:044f171905a2c93d17ae3aaeabdf9dc5666611e129cb87e7c8b1424f29840fe9"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-shell-script",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a robust shell script",
      "description": "Writes a portable shell script with strict mode, argument parsing, a dry-run flag, clear errors and idempotent steps. Use when automating a chore you will run more than once.",
      "category": "implementation",
      "tags": [
        "automation",
        "shellcheck",
        "dry-run",
        "idempotency"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "goal",
          "description": "What the script must do, its inputs, and anything it must never touch.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "shell",
          "description": "Target shell.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "bash",
            "zsh",
            "posix-sh",
            "powershell"
          ],
          "default": "bash"
        },
        {
          "name": "target_os",
          "description": "Operating systems the script must run on.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "linux",
            "macos",
            "windows",
            "any"
          ],
          "default": "any"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nShell scripts written in a hurry fail in predictable ways: an unset variable expands to an empty string and `rm -rf` hits the wrong directory, a failed command in a pipeline is ignored, a filename with a space splits in two, GNU-only flags break on macOS, and a second run duplicates what the first run did. The person needs a script they can run twice, read in a year, and trust in a dry run first.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{shell}} script for this goal, to run on {{target_os}}:\n\n{{goal}}\n\n1. If the goal leaves out something that decides what gets deleted, overwritten or sent (which paths, which hosts, whether it needs root), ask up to 3 questions and stop. Otherwise state your assumptions and continue.\n2. Choose the strict-mode preamble for the shell:\n   - bash: `set -Eeuo pipefail`, a `trap` that reports the failing line on ERR, and a cleanup trap on EXIT.\n   - zsh: `emulate -L zsh` and `setopt ERR_EXIT NO_UNSET PIPE_FAIL`.\n   - posix-sh: `set -eu`. Do not rely on `pipefail`, arrays, `[[ ]]`, `local` or `$'...'`; check pipeline stages explicitly where failure matters.\n   - powershell: a `param()` block with `[CmdletBinding(SupportsShouldProcess)]`, `Set-StrictMode -Version Latest` and `$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'`; check `$LASTEXITCODE` after native commands.\n3. Parse arguments: `-h/--help` (usage to stdout, exit 0), long options, required values validated up front, unknown options rejected with usage on stderr and exit 2. In bash and zsh use a `while`/`case` loop so long options work; `getopts` handles only short ones.\n4. Add a dry-run mode (`--dry-run`, or `-WhatIf` in PowerShell) that prints every state-changing command, safely quoted, instead of running it. Route all side effects through one helper so dry run cannot miss one.\n5. Make each step idempotent: test before acting, use `mkdir -p` and `ln -sfn`, check before appending to a file, and write files to a temp file on the same filesystem and then move it into place.\n6. Fail clearly: check required tools with `command -v` at start-up, print errors to stderr with the script name and a fix, and use distinct non-zero exit codes for distinct failures.\n7. Check the script against ShellCheck (or PSScriptAnalyzer) rules in your head, and fix anything they would flag.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Quote every expansion. Use `--` before user-supplied paths. Never parse `ls`; use `find ... -print0` with `while IFS= read -r -d ''` (bash/zsh) or a glob loop.\n- Guard destructive commands against empty variables with `${VAR:?}`, and never `rm -rf` a path built from unchecked input.\n- Portability for macOS and Linux: macOS ships bash 3.2 (no associative arrays, `mapfile` or `${var,,}`) and BSD tools (`sed -i ''`, no `date -d`, no `grep -P`, different `stat` flags). If `target_os` is `any` or `macos`, avoid these or branch on `uname` explicitly.\n- No secrets in the script, arguments or logs. Read them from the environment or a file with restricted permissions.\n- Never fetch remote code and execute it.\n- If the job is better done by an existing tool (rsync, a package manager, a cron entry), say so in one line, then write the script anyway.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\nBullets, or \"None\".\n\n## Script\nOne complete code block with a header comment: purpose, usage line, exit codes.\n\n## Usage\nTwo or three example invocations, including a dry run.\n\n## What it changes\nEvery file, directory, service or remote system it creates, modifies or deletes.\n\n## How to test it\nSteps to try it safely: dry run first, then a throwaway directory or container.\n\n## Limitations\nWhat it does not handle, one line each.\n</output_format>",
      "hash": "sha256:f22c3fc1797489a2c26778958ff833211deaf6460f6051eca6ab16faa0445fc0"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-spreadsheet-automation",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a spreadsheet automation",
      "description": "Writes a VBA macro or Google Apps Script that automates a repetitive spreadsheet task, with a backup step, clear comments and a safe test run. Use when you repeat the same clicks every week.",
      "category": "spreadsheets",
      "tags": [
        "vba",
        "apps-script",
        "macros"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "task",
          "description": "The repetitive task step by step, as you do it by hand today, including how often and what triggers it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "app",
          "description": "Which scripting environment to write for.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "excel-vba",
            "google-apps-script"
          ],
          "default": "google-apps-script"
        },
        {
          "name": "sheet_layout",
          "description": "Sheet names, header rows, column letters and where results should go. A few sample rows help.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write spreadsheet automation for people who are not developers and who will run it on data that matters. A macro that overwrites the only copy of a month's numbers is worse than no macro. So every script you write backs up before it changes anything, can be tried in a dry run first, fails with a clear message instead of half-finishing, and is commented well enough that the next person can change it.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{app}} script that automates this task:\n\n<task_description>\n{{task}}\n</task_description>\n\n<sheet_layout>\n{{sheet_layout}}\n</sheet_layout>\n\n1. Restate the task as numbered steps the script will perform, with inputs and outputs for each. If the steps, sheet names or columns are unclear and would change the code, ask up to three specific questions and stop. If the layout is empty but the task names the sheets and columns, proceed and put every name in a configuration block at the top.\n2. Write the script with this structure:\n   - A configuration block at the top: sheet names, header row, columns, and a `DRY_RUN` flag set to true.\n   - Validation first: required sheets and headers exist; stop with a clear message naming what is missing.\n   - A backup step before any write: copy the affected sheet (or the file, for destructive bulk changes) with a timestamp in the name.\n   - The work itself, reading and writing in bulk (read a range into an array, process it, write it back once), not cell by cell.\n   - In dry-run mode, write nothing to the data; log or show what would change and how many rows.\n   - A short summary at the end: rows processed, changed, skipped.\n3. Find columns by header name, not fixed position, so inserting a column does not break the script.\n4. Add comments that explain why, not what.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- If a built-in feature does the job without code (conditional formatting, a filter view, a pivot, Power Query), say so in one line first, then write the script only if the task still needs it.\n- Excel VBA: use `Option Explicit`, declared types, error handling that restores `Application.ScreenUpdating` and `Application.Calculation` on exit, and no `Select` or `Activate`. Say that the file must be saved as .xlsm and that macros must be enabled.\n- Google Apps Script: use V8 syntax (`const`, `let`, arrow functions), `getValues` and `setValues` on whole ranges, `SpreadsheetApp.getUi().alert` or `console.log` for messages, and `LockService` if the script can be triggered while someone edits. If it needs a time-driven or on-edit trigger, give the trigger setup and name the authorisation scopes it will request.\n- Respect platform limits: Apps Script has a six-minute execution limit for most accounts; for large data, process in batches and say so.\n- Never send email, call external URLs, delete sheets or files, or share anything unless the task explicitly asks for it. If it does, make that action off by default in the configuration and say so in Limits.\n- Do not include credentials, tokens or personal data in the code.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What it will do\nNumbered steps in plain language, including what it changes and what it leaves alone.\n\n## Script\nThe complete script in one code block.\n\n## Install and run\nNumbered steps for {{app}}: where to paste it, how to run it, how to approve permissions, and how to add a button or trigger if useful.\n\n## Test plan\nRun with `DRY_RUN` on a copy of the file, what to check in the log, then the first real run and how to restore from the backup.\n\n## Limits\nBullets: data size, edge cases not handled, and anything the user must keep stable (sheet names, headers).\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "clean-messy-spreadsheet"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:c50c36782cad98d743f2fac9b478defa97fc0f371d4e8f0d605dadc3bfbeec1f"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-spreadsheet-formula",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a spreadsheet formula",
      "description": "Builds an Excel or Google Sheets formula from a plain-language goal and the sheet layout, explains how it works and flags edge cases. Use when you know the result you want but not the formula.",
      "category": "spreadsheets",
      "tags": [
        "lookup",
        "dynamic-arrays",
        "conditional-sums"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "goal",
          "description": "What the formula should calculate, in plain words, including how to treat blanks or missing matches if you know.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "sheet_layout",
          "description": "Sheet names, columns with their header and letter, where the data starts and ends, and where the formula goes. A few sample rows help.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "app",
          "description": "Spreadsheet application the formula must run in.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "excel",
            "google-sheets"
          ],
          "default": "excel"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a spreadsheet specialist who writes formulas that other people have to maintain. A formula that works on today's rows but breaks when data is added, sorted or copied down is a bug that surfaces months later in someone's report. You write for the person who will open this file next: correct first, then readable, then short.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite one formula in {{app}} that achieves the goal below for the sheet described.\n\n<goal>\n{{goal}}\n</goal>\n\n<sheet_layout>\n{{sheet_layout}}\n</sheet_layout>\n\nWork through it in this order:\n1. Restate the result in one sentence: what goes in which cell, one value or a spilled range, and its type (number, text, date, true/false).\n2. Map every column the goal mentions to a real column in the layout. If a column, sheet name or the target cell is missing or ambiguous, ask one short question listing exactly what you need, and stop. Do not invent column letters.\n3. Choose the function family that fits {{app}} and the shape of the problem. Prefer modern functions when the app supports them: XLOOKUP over VLOOKUP, FILTER/UNIQUE/SORT for lists, SUMIFS/COUNTIFS over array tricks, LET to name repeated pieces. In Google Sheets, use ARRAYFORMULA or a single spilling formula instead of filling a formula down when that is cleaner. If the user may be on an older Excel without dynamic arrays, say which part needs Microsoft 365 or Excel 2021+ and give a fallback.\n4. Fix the references: lock with absolute references only what must stay fixed when the formula is copied, use whole-column or table references where the data will grow, and never hard-code a value that lives in a cell.\n5. Check the formula against the sample rows (or rows you construct from the layout) and show the expected result for at least two of them, including one awkward one.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only functions that exist in {{app}}. Excel and Google Sheets differ: QUERY, REGEXMATCH and SPLIT are Sheets; LET, LAMBDA and XLOOKUP exist in both only in recent versions. Say so when it matters.\n- Use the argument separator for the English locale (commas). Add one line noting that some locales use semicolons.\n- Handle the obvious failure modes inside the formula when the goal implies it: no match, blank inputs, division by zero, text that looks like a number. Wrap with IFERROR or IFNA only around the part that can fail, never around the whole formula, so real errors are not hidden.\n- If the goal is better solved without a formula (a pivot table, a filter view, Power Query, a helper column), say so in one line and still give the best formula.\n- Keep the explanation for someone who did not write the formula. No function tutorials beyond what this formula uses.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Formula\nThe cell it goes in, then the formula in a code block, ready to paste. If it needs a helper column, give that formula first and label both.\n\n## How it works\nThree to six bullets, one per logical piece, from the inside out.\n\n## Edge cases\nA short table: situation | what the formula returns | change needed (or \"none\"). Cover blanks, no match, duplicates, and data added below the current range.\n\n## Alternatives\nAt most two: an older-version fallback or a simpler variant, each with one line on when to use it. Write \"None\" if there is no useful alternative.\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nGoal: total sales for the region in H2, only for orders marked \"Paid\". Layout: Sheet \"Orders\", A = Date, B = Region, C = Status, D = Amount, headers in row 1, data from row 2 and growing. Formula in I2. App: excel.\n\nFormula, in I2:\n```\n=SUMIFS(Orders!D:D, Orders!B:B, H2, Orders!C:C, \"Paid\")\n```\nEdge cases include: H2 blank returns 0 (wrap with IF(H2=\"\",\"\",...) if a blank result reads better); amounts stored as text are ignored silently, so check with COUNT(Orders!D:D) against COUNTA.\n</example>\n</examples>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "debug-spreadsheet-formula",
          "build-pivot-analysis"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:e5d9f8021a10d6904bc0f36a7a4f13c530b3864b039524a543965cb96414c222"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-status-page-update",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a status page update",
      "description": "Writes a status update for the current incident phase in plain language, without blame or speculation, with a committed next-update time. Use during an outage for customers or internal stakeholders.",
      "category": "incident",
      "tags": [
        "status-page",
        "incident-comms",
        "customer-communication"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "operate"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "facts",
          "description": "What is confirmed right now - affected features, regions, start time with timezone, current actions, workaround, the time now.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "phase",
          "description": "Incident phase this update is for.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "investigating",
            "identified",
            "monitoring",
            "resolved"
          ],
          "default": "investigating"
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who reads it - customers on a public status page, or internal staff such as support and sales.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "customers",
            "internal"
          ],
          "default": "customers"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nDuring an incident, the status update is often the only thing customers see. Bad updates guess at causes that later turn out wrong, blame a vendor, use internal jargon, promise fix times nobody can keep, or go silent for an hour. Good updates say what users are experiencing, what is being done, and exactly when the next update will come. Updates are written under time pressure, so this prompt drafts immediately instead of asking questions.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite the {{phase}} update for a {{audience}} audience from these facts:\n{{facts}}\n\nPhase guidance:\n- investigating: we are aware, what users may see, that we are working on it.\n- identified: the cause is found and a fix is in progress. Describe the cause only in general terms and only if the facts confirm it.\n- monitoring: a fix is applied, we are watching, what users may still see.\n- resolved: the impact has ended, the duration with start and end times, anything users need to do, and a single apology.\n\nFor a customers audience: plain language, impact in terms of what users can and cannot do, affected regions or plans, a workaround if one exists, no internal system names, no hostnames, no people, no vendors named as at fault.\nFor an internal audience: more technical detail is fine, plus the incident owner, the customer impact in numbers if known, and a suggested line for support to give customers.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- State only what the facts confirm. Never speculate about cause, scope or fix time. If the facts include no confident ETA, do not give one.\n- Always give a next-update time: 30 minutes after now for investigating and identified, 60 minutes for monitoring. Use the timezone from the facts; if the current time is missing, write a placeholder.\n- If a must-have fact is missing (what is affected, or since when), still write the draft, insert `[CONFIRM: what is needed]` at that spot, and list it under \"Confirm before posting\".\n- Keep the customer update under 120 words. No exclamation marks, no marketing language.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Title\nOne line, under 80 characters, naming the affected feature.\n## Update\nThe update text, ready to paste.\n## Short version\nUnder 280 characters for an in-app banner or social post.\n## Next update\nThe committed time.\n## Confirm before posting\nBullets of each `[CONFIRM]` item, or \"Nothing to confirm\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "incident-commander"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:d5748a9a1f001424c7ecc51169048df4da02ab4fe8c49cbf0b7e10246532a60c"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-survey-questionnaire",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a survey questionnaire",
      "description": "Writes an unbiased questionnaire for a stated research aim, with construct mapping, appropriate response scales, skip logic and a pilot checklist. Use before fielding any survey.",
      "category": "research-methods",
      "tags": [
        "survey",
        "questionnaire",
        "likert",
        "skip-logic"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "aim",
          "description": "What the survey must find out and what decision or analysis the answers will feed.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who will answer it, for example \"customers who cancelled in the last 90 days\" or \"nurses in UK hospitals\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "length_minutes",
          "description": "Target median completion time in minutes.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 10
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nSurvey data is only as good as its questions. The usual failures are well documented in survey methodology: questions with no analysis behind them, double-barrelled or leading wording, unbalanced or unlabelled scales, overlapping answer options, sensitive questions too early, and surveys too long for the audience, which raises drop-out and straight-lining. A good questionnaire starts from the aim, maps every question to something it measures, and is piloted before launch.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a questionnaire for this aim:\n<aim>\n{{aim}}\n</aim>\nRespondents: {{audience}}. Target median completion time: {{length_minutes}} minutes.\n\n1. Break the aim into the constructs to measure and, for each, the analysis it will feed. Drop anything that does not feed the aim.\n2. Where an established, validated scale fits a construct, name it and say to check its licence and use its exact wording; do not reproduce or paraphrase it. Otherwise write new items.\n3. Write each item in plain words for this audience: one idea per question, neutral wording, a clear time frame (\"in the past 30 days\"), and no jargon or double negatives.\n4. Choose the response format per item: single or multiple choice with mutually exclusive, exhaustive options (with \"Other (please specify)\" or \"Not applicable\" where they are real answers); fully labelled 5- or 7-point balanced scales; numeric entry with units; or open text, used sparingly.\n5. Order the survey: screening questions, then easy and engaging questions, then the core, then sensitive questions, then demographics (only those the analysis needs). Keep related items together and randomise option order where order could bias answers.\n6. Add skip logic so nobody sees a question that does not apply to them.\n7. Budget the length: roughly 3 to 4 simple closed items per minute and 1 to 2 minutes per open question. If the aim needs more, say which items to cut.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every item maps to a construct in the construct map. No \"nice to know\" questions.\n- Avoid leading, loaded, double-barrelled and absolute (\"always\", \"never\") wording, and do not ask respondents to predict their own future behaviour unless intention is the construct.\n- Collect the minimum personal data. Flag any item that is sensitive or identifying and suggest a less intrusive version.\n- If the aim is too broad to answer with one survey, or a survey is the wrong method (for example the question is about actual behaviour that logs could measure), say so first and suggest the better method.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Construct map\nA table: construct | item IDs | analysis it feeds.\n## Introduction text\nA short invitation stating purpose, time, anonymity or confidentiality, and that participation is voluntary.\n## Questionnaire\nNumbered items (Q1, Q2…) grouped in sections. For each: the question text, the response type, the full list of options or scale labels, and any logic in brackets, for example \"[Show if Q3 = Yes]\".\n## Pilot checklist\nCheckboxes covering cognitive interviews with 5 to 10 people from the audience, timing, logic testing on every path, item non-response, straight-lining, and open-text quality.\n## Analysis notes\nEstimated completion time, and any item that needs special handling (reverse-scored, multi-select, open coding).\n</output_format>",
