    {
      "id": "write-terraform-module",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a Terraform module",
      "description": "Writes a reusable Terraform module with typed, validated variables, secure defaults, documented outputs and an example. Use when wrapping cloud resources for other teams to consume.",
      "category": "devops",
      "tags": [
        "iac",
        "terraform-module",
        "least-privilege",
        "secure-defaults"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "resource_goal",
          "description": "What the module should provision and for whom, e.g. \"private S3 bucket for app uploads with lifecycle rules\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "cloud",
          "description": "Target provider.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "aws",
            "gcp",
            "azure",
            "cloudflare"
          ],
          "default": "aws"
        },
        {
          "name": "constraints",
          "description": "Org rules to honour, such as naming, tagging, regions, compliance or provider versions.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA Terraform module is an API. Its variables are the inputs other teams depend on and its outputs are the contract they build on, so changing either later is a breaking change. Generated modules usually fail in the same ways: untyped `any` variables, hard-coded regions and account IDs, provider blocks inside the module, `count` where `for_each` belongs, and insecure defaults such as public access or wildcard IAM. The target here is a module a platform team would publish to its internal registry.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a reusable Terraform module for {{cloud}} that does this:\n{{resource_goal}}\n{{#constraints}}\nHonour these constraints:\n{{constraints}}\n{{/constraints}}\n\n1. If the goal leaves open a decision that changes the design (single or multi-region, public or private, whether data must survive `terraform destroy`), ask up to 3 questions and stop. If the gap is a detail, choose the safe default and record it under Assumptions.\n2. Draw the boundary: one cohesive purpose. Take shared things (VPC or network IDs, KMS keys, DNS zones) as inputs instead of creating them.\n3. Variables: explicit types (object types with `optional()` attributes rather than `any`), a description on each, `validation` blocks for formats, ranges and allowed values, `sensitive = true` where it applies. Required inputs have no default; everything else defaults to the safe choice.\n4. Resources: encryption at rest on, public access off, least-privilege IAM with no wildcard action on a wildcard resource, deletion protection or `prevent_destroy` on stateful resources where the provider supports it, and tags or labels merged from a `tags` variable.\n5. Use `for_each` keyed by stable names for collections, so removing one item does not recreate the others.\n6. Pin `required_version` and providers with pessimistic constraints in `versions.tf`. Never put a `provider` or `backend` block in the module.\n7. Output what callers need (IDs, ARNs or self-links, endpoints), each with a description; mark secrets `sensitive`.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only resources and arguments that exist in the current {{cloud}} provider. If you are unsure an argument exists in the pinned version, say so under Assumptions instead of guessing silently.\n- No hard-coded account IDs, regions, CIDRs, image IDs or names. They come from variables or data sources.\n- No provisioners or `local-exec` unless the goal cannot be met otherwise; explain why if you use one.\n- The code must pass `terraform fmt` and `terraform validate`. You cannot run them here, so do not claim they pass.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\nBullets: each default you chose and why. \"None\" if the goal settled everything.\n## Files\nOne fenced `hcl` block per file, headed by its path: `versions.tf`, `variables.tf`, `main.tf`, `outputs.tf`, `examples/basic/main.tf`.\n## README\nAn inputs table (name, type, default, description), an outputs table, and a short usage paragraph.\n## Verify\nThe commands to run (`terraform fmt -check`, `terraform validate`, `terraform plan` on the example, plus a static scanner such as tflint or checkov) and what to look for in the plan.\n</output_format>",
